#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193672} :activity/id #uuid "c9c41155-2ae1-5832-8b1b-927ecf980d8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048630 :topic/id #uuid "b01dd6bb-9a15-5222-a046-9d5a3d2d57bb" :topic/math-academy-id 4096} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192508 :question/id #uuid "89d0136d-63d1-540b-a3be-acff2d0bbbb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11027"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323712} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436610}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8f9e1a5f-2ddb-5dcf-bdb9-12c97e29ab65" :step/math-academy-id 41900} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192509 :question/id #uuid "0fd784a0-5146-5f7b-9936-8d3bc43d7062" :question/math-academy-id "e-11028"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436611}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "d18aca64-f3ac-5a3a-837c-b8cb8fa44772" :step/math-academy-id 41903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193680}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192514 :question/id #uuid "fc8ba431-5a97-5f57-9a83-fdcf4ac98d39" :question/math-academy-id "e-11048"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235542} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278243} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436615}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2a951cc5-70fa-5cd6-a379-4cfe861c71d4" :step/math-academy-id 41983 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193890 :tutorial/content "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66f2199e-b3f8-5590-80f3-bbac79027096" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6937 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c3b7bd9a-f6d4-521a-ab11-4ff2cecfbe07" :step/math-academy-id 41989 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193680 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192518 :question/id #uuid "6f7bdbcf-fb8e-5c70-b368-4e73cc94bdb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11112"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278247} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436614}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "4bae0aea-9643-5e83-9379-45ac0682c9ca" :step/math-academy-id 42238 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193698}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193907 :tutorial/content "We can also compute partial derivatives of functions with three or even more variables. \n\nFor example, suppose we have the function $f(x,y,z).$ We find its partial derivatives as follows:\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x},$ we differentiate with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z},$ we differentiate with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ *and* $y$ as constants.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c87e2fef-e77f-5c67-a502-0d900d85ee40" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7692 :tutorial/title "Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"} :step/id #uuid "1014e39c-1f7c-5989-8916-134b9e90b165" :step/math-academy-id 44640 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193660}}] :activity/title "Computing Partial Derivatives Using the Rules of Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192923}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125269} :activity/id #uuid "c9d087b0-e0c7-5257-8039-6b926add5c2f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781 :topic/id #uuid "be3a8d83-d961-5a49-82b6-e2c59f1bcd4b" :topic/math-academy-id 1933} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125724 :tutorial/content "Just like we can compute second derivatives of single-variable functions, we can also compute second derivatives of multivariable functions.\n\nFor example, consider the two-variable function\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nIts two first-order partial derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}+y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2xy+x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f_x(x,y)$ and $f_y(x,y)$ are themselves functions that are differentiable with respect to both $x$ and $y,$ we can compute the **second-order partial derivatives** as well. \n\n- The partial derivatives of $f_x(x,y)$ with respect to $x$ and $y,$ denoted $f_{xx}$ and $f_{xy}$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y))={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y))=2y+xy{e}^{xy}+{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The partial derivatives of $f_y(x,y)$ with respect to $x$ and $y,$ denoted $f_{yx}$ and $f_{yy}$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y))=2y+xy{e}^{xy}+{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y))=2x+{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe partial derivatives $f_{xy}$ and $f_{yx}$ are called **mixed partial derivatives** because they involve taking the partial derivatives with respect to different variables, $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe following alternative notations are also widely used to represent second-order partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{x}^{2}}(f) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}={f}_{xx}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{y}^{2}}(f)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}={f}_{yy} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂y\\,∂x}(f) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y\\,∂x}={f}_{xy}\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x\\,∂y}(f)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x\\,∂y}={f}_{yx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In our example, the two mixed partial derivatives were equal:\n\n$$\nf_{xy}(x,y) = 2y + xye^{xy} + e^{xy} = f_{yx}(x,y)\n$$\n\nLater, we will see that under rather mild conditions on $f,$ we almost always have that $f_{xy}=f_{yx}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "feb54aa0-17fb-5468-8b99-14174a6bd059" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2397 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0eaae658-c43c-5756-80c4-c4462162b555" :step/math-academy-id 10558 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124440 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124244 :question/id #uuid "0a8f5141-941d-5059-9d9f-bf9e5b09096f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3127"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ea31275-77bd-59c7-8e1d-96e2e4b252a4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436905} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436907}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Mixed Second-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "4f4f64aa-ab4f-5abb-8d9c-ca2aa2790b64" :step/math-academy-id 10559 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124441 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124245 :question/id #uuid "e16f6686-dffb-554e-a50a-8760e818439f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3129"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a0bfe-7c69-5ad0-ad1d-aa8692dae673" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268921} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436904} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436908}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Expressions Involving Second-Order Partial Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "a25341f7-8d06-561b-9802-42d2f9c792e8" :step/math-academy-id 10563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125725 :tutorial/content "We can also define other **higher order partial derivatives** such as $f_{xxy}, \\, f_{xxyy}, \\, f_{xyxy},$ etc. \n\nFor instance, the function $f(x,y,z)= z\\cos(xy)$ has the first-order partial derivative\n\n$$\nf_y(x,y,z) = -xz \\sin(xy)\n$$\n\nand the second-order partial derivative\n\n$$\nf_{yx}(x,y,z)= -x yz \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - z\\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}.\n$$\n\nIf we differentiate again, with respect to $x,$ then we get the **third-order partial derivative** $f_{yxx}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xyz(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y)-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\cdot y \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)-2yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, to compute a $n$th-order partial derivative, we need to compute a sequence of $n$ individual derivatives." :tutorial/id #uuid "e256986d-ca95-50b3-a16c-0f46005b5c66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2398 :tutorial/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives for Functions of  More Than Two Variables"} :step/id #uuid "f8b4cf47-e81c-516c-8eef-7d544743362a" :step/math-academy-id 10566 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124442 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124246 :question/id #uuid "a41bfb06-4372-5124-b910-4b35f19d148b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83724b66-0f15-5b94-9576-76e9dfd56d38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483391}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Higher-Order Derivatives"} :step/id #uuid "25a77626-7a96-5d1b-b087-a01012cbeb91" :step/math-academy-id 10567}] :activity/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124726}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422014} :activity/id #uuid "59407835-7bfb-56b8-b3fd-56d3687982eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781 :topic/id #uuid "be3a8d83-d961-5a49-82b6-e2c59f1bcd4b" :topic/math-academy-id 1933} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422012 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c70e303-c80e-51d2-8821-2af0cf8b3950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421995} {:answer/id #uuid "92245356-2a0e-55cd-8484-17313be9c3ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422003} {:answer/id #uuid "992b5993-493b-561e-9033-5437b3de59e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422004} {:answer/id #uuid "ca872c1a-6f89-5f8b-8336-d7cb854e1bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422005} {:answer/id #uuid "d1afdc0c-1cf1-54cf-9c95-d81dfb59dc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422006}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422006} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a95cb7d-93f6-5084-ade9-1c85a3b6b1f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdce2d33-30de-52e0-89c4-590147a8c4ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-80479" :question/problem "Calculate ${g}_{xxy}(x,y)$ given that $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(({x}^{2}+y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${g}_{xxy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xxy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{xx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "25f65be6-b106-53d1-9640-27a18badcd80" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422015}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422010 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b7ad2e-54cc-50e3-9ca3-0b8efd17be6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4y+{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421994} {:answer/id #uuid "1f109dc7-15d8-584f-8e2b-595762c1a061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4xy+y{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421996} {:answer/id #uuid "44455996-432d-5a3d-8b99-8ba088ea40bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2xy+{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421998} {:answer/id #uuid "5e886d04-bc8d-58c5-9141-d4629aa452fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421999} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c5be38-2517-52b4-89a2-e748dfb3f71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y+x+{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421994} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dfaa29-640a-5223-927a-6d7e182b7cd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "11508c24-c4aa-589a-b2fa-c4b12396bfe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65070" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot {y}^{2}+y\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =2x{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+y{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot 2y+1\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =4xy+{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot y+{e}^{x} \\\\  & =4y+{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4932cd51-46e5-51d8-99f7-f0e3336e3476" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422012}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422014 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01b1340c-9ea7-510e-a7fe-a5318d1aa165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421993} {:answer/id #uuid "2ea483d9-4903-536f-a106-b11346fafb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421997} {:answer/id #uuid "646a4289-2437-5a76-800d-038e82b06562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422000} {:answer/id #uuid "654452d8-a947-5e7b-b22c-8a13e2ef81d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422001} {:answer/id #uuid "6b0fbde1-6b7d-56c5-b32e-24ed9e9df6a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421997} :answer-field/id #uuid "f436de7c-3f9a-5c8c-8ef3-8a7a55ee418d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9fe7b82e-efd4-53ad-afa8-cb700e6400e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-35936" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y)+{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "73d31843-2db2-5471-8b39-85a8ef5439bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422015 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573ca17f-8b55-50aa-b136-59bd043a6536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266921} {:answer/id #uuid "5df633bf-d9fe-5623-9aed-bd0cc78f2d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266974} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc7ddd0-7d66-560f-b925-d71f0a4f1da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266989} {:answer/id #uuid "75e2d132-79e6-565d-b0a0-3480a84f1042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267184} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6cc2bf-6cc9-5989-82ab-f94ca56c41d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0722938f-1bbb-5341-b742-6808aa83c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aaf83dc-c135-54d5-b395-8dc2f637ad0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132894" :question/problem "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)-{g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})-{g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "999e1519-06f2-527e-a6e9-193c3c031bfa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124939 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9137d290-41b5-56f3-91a0-1bd21a361f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122235} {:answer/id #uuid "9a8c61f9-4828-5963-94b5-9c4024267fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122236} {:answer/id #uuid "fb781ced-0c22-5194-a9bf-5a48f6b0b287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122237} {:answer/id #uuid "54475861-eeaf-5969-a8d8-c01e85facc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122238} {:answer/id #uuid "74a0e08c-a99c-5a0d-80ac-df9b1bbcc42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186122236} :answer-field/id #uuid "0485544a-7efe-5a7c-a375-d04fc25e6208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ca6dfc54-54dd-5d46-99aa-e23ca5426a6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35969" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy) \\\\  & =2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}_{xyx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xy}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xyx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ed57f383-5736-5d28-bb66-05e9dcdf8f3a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422013}}] :activity/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421992}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483451} :activity/id #uuid "5b774f06-abdb-5ce8-b0a4-4f02fda3281c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781 :topic/id #uuid "be3a8d83-d961-5a49-82b6-e2c59f1bcd4b" :topic/math-academy-id 1933} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268920 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "573ca17f-8b55-50aa-b136-59bd043a6536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266921} {:answer/id #uuid "5df633bf-d9fe-5623-9aed-bd0cc78f2d1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266974} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc7ddd0-7d66-560f-b925-d71f0a4f1da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}+\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266989} {:answer/id #uuid "75e2d132-79e6-565d-b0a0-3480a84f1042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\pi -\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267184} {:answer/id #uuid "aa6cc2bf-6cc9-5989-82ab-f94ca56c41d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267685}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266974} :answer-field/id #uuid "0722938f-1bbb-5341-b742-6808aa83c68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aaf83dc-c135-54d5-b395-8dc2f637ad0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132894" :question/problem "Find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)-{g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ given that ${g}_{x}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)$ and ${g}_{y}(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{x}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({g}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${g}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({g}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we evaluate ${g}_{xx}(x,y)$ and ${g}_{yy}(x,y)$ at $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{xx}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})-{g}_{yy}(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0156998a-6e16-5b9a-9f34-3fa9b3ed9afa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483504}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0edad87c-2a7e-5ce2-948c-23ebc4486f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482835} {:answer/id #uuid "87f3dc21-f9fe-515d-b7a6-aa328708748c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483051} {:answer/id #uuid "8bd1a042-f0e1-5280-a93f-79b975c4ee9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483058} {:answer/id #uuid "a5057d38-1225-50a0-b6ad-25ed31fa235e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483092} {:answer/id #uuid "ed0e24a1-111c-5e17-8f98-c82fe143a3a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483210}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483058} :answer-field/id #uuid "58cd874b-d401-5dae-a003-642f47b6d5e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a8c3fd0e-01dd-58d7-b5d0-e6ecb2b5f479" :question/math-academy-id "q-65054" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{zyx}(1,1,-1)$ for $f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{z}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =xy\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{x+y+z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{zy}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{z}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zy}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{z}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{xy}{x+y+z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot (x+y+z)-xy\\cdot (1)}{(x+y+z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-xy}{(x+y+z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz}{(x+y+z)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{zyx}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{zy}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zyx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{zy}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz}{(x+y+z)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+z)\\cdot (x+y+z)^{2}-({x}^{2}+xz)\\cdot (2(x+y+z)\\cdot 1)}{(x+y+z)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+z)(x+y+z)-2({x}^{2}+xz)}{(x+y+z)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{zyx}(x,y,z)$ at $(1,1,-1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{zyx}(1,1,-1) & =\\frac{(2(1)+(-1))(1+1+(-1))-2({1}^{2}+1(-1))}{(1+1+(-1))^{3}}=\\frac{(1)(1)-2(0)}{(1)^{3}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "345a4ba2-99a5-54c6-920b-943ebcfbdddc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483455}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335788 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0548138d-e543-5a54-a872-c6505ae35f31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333031} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd21545-2656-5f34-8899-9e63ce0f1a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333573} {:answer/id #uuid "56f12986-c08d-5855-9c32-e38ca3128af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333777} {:answer/id #uuid "7279d9a1-e1cd-5962-b045-74040fa8edd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{y}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334026} {:answer/id #uuid "810185ca-f52f-5e5b-a85b-df54ad11e6a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4+y}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334135}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333031} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a6c17f-ff74-5dd2-941a-c73839c7e4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ab0f3803-2840-52ec-8174-767389c2fcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-65071" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}_{yxx}(x,y)$ given that $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =2y\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${f}_{yxx}(x,y)$ by differentiating ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{yx}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4y}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot x-4y\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4y}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3fdcb306-4c4d-55b0-afc4-b7f0ddec864f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483474}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483474 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28fd9f48-019d-5f27-baf0-6a1841493cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382381} {:answer/id #uuid "2ecd2278-324d-5173-a901-03897d9ade19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-x(y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382382} {:answer/id #uuid "1f517a1e-8bfc-56fe-bed2-6c99b6f579a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-4{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382383} {:answer/id #uuid "fd42600b-5686-573f-98cb-1cd8e2a97d37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-(2y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382384} {:answer/id #uuid "b86868bc-6931-5551-b2ee-dc760e6b939c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2y(y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382385}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382385} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a514deb-d87a-5e16-bf0c-b2bff11c7a40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b4318c10-0a8e-5140-827c-8a0c895809ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-80466" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y)$ given that  ${f}_{y}(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}_{yx}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find ${f}_{yy}(x,y)$ we differentiate the given first-order partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yx}(x,y)+{f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{y}^{2})-2y(y+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7500e142-5145-59e0-8086-fdd6d710a0a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483431}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483504 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268923 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00508df2-67fc-5789-9478-c3e2d42771e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266152} {:answer/id #uuid "44da4fc7-1df8-536a-a190-f8c23a3e32f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266758} {:answer/id #uuid "63a9b984-28e7-58d3-8128-0a6d4ca98f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267021} {:answer/id #uuid "88f1885b-f866-5cf3-96ea-874e43e6a210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267363} {:answer/id #uuid "8ebfbd8f-e225-52f2-af81-73501dcd099d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267416}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266152} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f17d030-5507-55e9-8f15-4689404588ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "01b2f238-4be0-502a-a439-500d85cd72b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132961" :question/problem "Evaluate ${f}_{xxz}(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\pi )$ for $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the first-order derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡xyz) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\cdot yz \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{xx}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{x}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({f}_{x}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot (-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)$ by differentiating ${f}_{xx}(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xxz}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({f}_{xx}(x,y,z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-{y}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}\\cdot (2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)+xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)) \\\\  & =-{y}^{2}z(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)+xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate ${f}_{xxz}(x,y,z)$ at $(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xxz}(1,\\frac{1}{2},\\pi ) & =-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi )+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi )) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ec92f449-cc5d-5eb7-8ed8-e8baf8365b8f"}] :activity/title "Higher-Order Partial Derivatives" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482804}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158351} :activity/id #uuid "980e0bea-1189-578f-9781-512b76215027" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859 :topic/id #uuid "c6e63ba3-f17e-5d7d-8b1e-f7ca5b0feff7" :topic/math-academy-id 2824} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158568 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate the total derivative of a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R.$ In this lesson, we will learn how to calculate its second derivative.\n\nFor a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n\n![](images/44/44a95860d7e7bd926f4d94c2ebb5b037e8537ac622f7c86b500aa06b9f959250.svg)\n\nthe total derivative of $f$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/e0/e0c9f72b67a531f992342e9f9e0b74f447de5e2b62c9fd81f08804096a912e67.svg)\n\nTo differentiate $f$ once more, we first need to take the transpose of the first derivative:\n\n![](images/4d/4d23282171c39149eeebd7301550ebdf1727b86736a57de6e5d7b6ea96891157.svg)\n\nTo calculate the **second derivative** of $f,$ we simply apply the definition of the total derivative to $\\left(\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)\\right)^T.$ By doing this, we get the following $n\\times n$ matrix:\n\n![](images/3d/3d62db0c4018fcce47077285aa05e6c4ea3c79e972ab87fccf5165a07083eda9.svg)\n\nNotice that this matrix is *symmetric* due to the equality of mixed partial derivatives. This becomes important later. \n\nThe matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x)$ is sometimes called the **Hessian matrix,** and its determinant, \n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det\\big( \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\big)\n$$\n\nis called the **Hessian determinant**.\n\nNote that in the case of a two-variable function $f(x,y),$ the second derivative is given by\n\n![](images/e8/e83195902ce2cdc2e0ff6c1f51ebc679a4bddd51fedd38c4bc6fddc4c73373dd.svg)\n\nIn this lesson, we'll primarily focus on two-variable functions $f(x,y).$ However, we should remember that the techniques discussed here can be applied to scalar functions with an arbitrary number of variables." :tutorial/id #uuid "79cc27de-f461-55d9-ab0f-3dd5a21dd4f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7795 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "bac39a49-196c-50bd-99ec-d3c5b74be977" :step/math-academy-id 45105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158352}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158352 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157000 :question/id #uuid "332b1d25-852f-51ad-9a38-62b562ae247f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12522"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c4234a-3aa1-5b6b-b68b-48e6aa2b0dfb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435394}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Function Given Its First Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "bdf28805-f615-5f91-bade-c3cb7038cfd2" :step/math-academy-id 45106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157001 :question/id #uuid "53ca572c-798a-5529-9702-df3291a2b500" :question/math-academy-id "e-12523"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527148}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Hessian Determinant"} :step/id #uuid "e732cfd5-6c10-55ef-b698-a932a1d233c3" :step/math-academy-id 45109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157002 :question/id #uuid "235325ea-4a42-5f63-974a-edd459a4a771" :question/math-academy-id "e-12524"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddf99849-1f2b-5c34-828c-164ad4058c92" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511545}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "8222059e-dad9-55b4-8e1d-ac327c86df18" :step/math-academy-id 45116 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158353}}] :activity/title "The Second Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157481}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481451} :activity/id #uuid "0d220057-0079-57e6-bb5c-eb3461a91e72" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859 :topic/id #uuid "c6e63ba3-f17e-5d7d-8b1e-f7ca5b0feff7" :topic/math-academy-id 2824} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481442 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237927 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62d58fc9-233d-5a98-9f47-8a3790a441f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236443} {:answer/id #uuid "804c671d-3217-5280-aa02-fe2a73812ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236615} {:answer/id #uuid "88c43743-4bdd-5d6d-8482-949f26f0705c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236672} {:answer/id #uuid "944e0f31-80d6-5eac-8960-7ada905bec43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236758} {:answer/id #uuid "d93bce69-5901-5324-bd97-83116ddf04a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186236672} :answer-field/id #uuid "77c1b5e6-8017-5a27-ad73-0d376c902134" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba2b3d0e-8f3a-5211-b064-2ffdb4af104d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102688" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))=-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & -{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,-1)=0\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "098d6e52-39b3-522b-84c5-e656d2477a6a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481463}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a863fee-48cb-518a-94c7-332f0d5dfb90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348671} {:answer/id #uuid "236352e1-e284-5137-93e8-01d0c0b82dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348749} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6da9cd-0b80-5391-a066-ff87ff441cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350254} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5d5ad-403a-5e9b-b47e-9495ed54eecd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350322} {:answer/id #uuid "da828216-6cb2-53d2-9a0c-74a4b4c5a870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f56bbbf-1a13-5c6d-a2d7-69c5469daa32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0dab700a-fa4f-5675-9473-37a2b428ee20" :question/math-academy-id "q-81023" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(x-1){y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2(x-1)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=4(x-1)y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=4(x-1)y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=2(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4(x-1)y \\\\ 4(x-1)y & 2(x-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4e33b2ed-2bc9-51ba-9064-eab461a12ff8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481414 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93574e00-8aff-52c2-8fef-8d24d76bb02a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481016} {:answer/id #uuid "aee9d420-4960-50a5-922a-c43137c76d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481067} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4a4294-c877-56f6-bfd0-3109ee79c13c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481097} {:answer/id #uuid "c00142a0-9805-5734-8860-3bf5256f853f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481104} {:answer/id #uuid "e631a5f0-8bc9-5fbb-b72c-fddfe67625ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481177}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481177} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cad98c5-f0af-530e-9545-f63d666cd3ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c02d4f47-274a-5db8-9775-386bac66028f" :question/math-academy-id "q-102684" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "63ea7e02-9f7a-5a09-b718-d06795e4cb97" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481442}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481455 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fca28ad-f59e-5a0a-bb4c-0662386d1e94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480829} {:answer/id #uuid "3075a971-db98-5b66-a3d1-9af12fdf7be7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480831} {:answer/id #uuid "8cb3995c-cc62-5136-82b2-472b0cb824be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481006} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ef3863-4e52-56dd-9984-66598e15e8ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481130} {:answer/id #uuid "dc10b03f-56c5-527c-94f1-47709864755a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481006} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c26c69-61cb-5d08-9210-43f100a3d7fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cd7463fc-6818-5da6-ab8a-b593c73ec670" :question/math-academy-id "q-102699" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)=xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y-2x{y}^{3} & x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x{y}^{3})=-2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x{y}^{3})=1-6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=-6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{y}^{3} & 1-6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 1-6x{y}^{2} & -6{x}^{2}y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,0).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "66302a80-8659-5359-be45-331fa2372867"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6147f2-99cb-5e0e-bfe7-1ab9fcb4bfaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430425} {:answer/id #uuid "3bd5f2d0-f063-5f9a-8554-0e38b366ddee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430426} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3b39c3-ebcc-501e-bf32-f62c8ab6fbca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430428} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8b4808-0307-5f43-b835-eaa5d7c78ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430432} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d3bd0b-8f50-5fda-8e7a-df552a252f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430436}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430425} :answer-field/id #uuid "57597420-75c9-5305-b6c7-82cc31ede092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f2460b1b-0ae3-5791-81e8-e644fac55aa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-102692" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x} & 2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x})=\\frac{2y}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2y}{x} & 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=(-1)\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2=-4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a882a718-46ae-5afd-bb1b-85e1be5639e2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481455}}] :activity/title "The Second Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480731}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527245} :activity/id #uuid "b90d5b5f-670a-59e8-878d-d35203a4b092" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859 :topic/id #uuid "c6e63ba3-f17e-5d7d-8b1e-f7ca5b0feff7" :topic/math-academy-id 2824} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527245 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394105 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e80e6d5-e332-5afa-ba42-d5007ebeac81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy} \\\\ {e}^{xy} & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394107} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe3ac6a-5289-56c8-a43c-0dd50e90253b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394108} {:answer/id #uuid "f7148b5f-bb4e-5a46-9866-cb5115f7f7af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}+xy \\\\ {e}^{xy}+xy & {e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394109} {:answer/id #uuid "16fdd3db-9b55-566b-86dc-7e9f1644148e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(2+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(2+xy) & {y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394110} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1384d2-d73e-5e0f-90d8-1bd54737465e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394111}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394108} :answer-field/id #uuid "bab53cf7-593b-54ae-b653-6ab7353d6f02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b55b18c-139d-56b5-94eb-26aeb293ef39" :question/math-academy-id "q-80947" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={e}^{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{e}^{xy})={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{xy})={e}^{xy}+xy\\,\\text{ }{e}^{xy} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})={e}^{xy}+xy\\,\\text{ }{e}^{xy}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{xy})={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}{e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}(1+xy) \\\\ {e}^{xy}(1+xy) & {x}^{2}{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3f710bff-ad8f-52f4-b629-314c5a07298d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527148 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376b6d35-c19b-56a0-93ca-9e64900695ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526607} {:answer/id #uuid "4d63a9d4-f48b-551f-a330-cb4773261398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526660} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd56c0-cc2d-5c47-9c19-0fd322aee1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526683} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6fbe7f-9b92-59d9-8aed-d5539feec274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526755} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b9d20a-a2bf-5378-921c-e265c88f998e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526755} :answer-field/id #uuid "905b588f-0f57-5923-a81f-afb037b87739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "33021c77-54f7-572b-82bd-06940f46518d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102691" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53935bee-25ea-5d48-bdee-5940866bf3d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527286}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007fcd15-219d-5a7b-979f-5872cbdb9291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} & \\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{x}{y} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430414} {:answer/id #uuid "157d76c9-2e6f-5cdb-897c-847f29e94391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{x}{y} & -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430417} {:answer/id #uuid "349923f1-1f51-5fc6-9589-ada6acd4c3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430423} {:answer/id #uuid "7529d158-9818-51d1-bf9e-38fc8f2bbd6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\frac{x}{y} & -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430429} {:answer/id #uuid "ea727c2d-0baf-5e14-8734-e9b51371b264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{x}{y}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430423} :answer-field/id #uuid "eca99a2b-d50a-519b-aef0-e2b86f3c4cf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "98de8580-5081-5c82-af81-6826d2545a45" :question/math-academy-id "q-102461" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-y)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}-1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y}-1)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e95f518-b55f-5878-93c7-26bfeaacd14e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351229 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0379a8f3-5c08-5a0e-9aa9-ee7247f5bb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348479} {:answer/id #uuid "0f2c18f5-f6c6-5cb7-9bd3-ef891a719882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348580} {:answer/id #uuid "235323a0-a480-5c75-9639-2308eb39664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2xy \\\\ 2xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348748} {:answer/id #uuid "3438c329-ab2c-5701-840d-707e4cfa738f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348915} {:answer/id #uuid "703d071f-0017-59ad-ade4-9601ec2c639e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349481} :answer-field/id #uuid "1892be87-58b9-5f45-a8a1-451663b051c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "235e87ab-40f0-5bca-9e1d-4479ce5e2de8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81119" :question/problem "Find ${f}^{″}(x,y)$ for the function $f(x,y)={y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}({x}^{2}-1))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{2} & 2y({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y({x}^{2}-1))=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2})=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y({x}^{2}-1))=2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2} & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2({x}^{2}-1)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e17d86c0-e66f-5653-a3ae-c011789c345f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527286 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a7ccfc6-c980-5f87-98b3-cb27c26de86d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281784} {:answer/id #uuid "1cc546d0-b0b6-5368-af23-5ec0c2c96352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281949} {:answer/id #uuid "48389a8d-c104-542e-8686-3b973c39c4df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282301} {:answer/id #uuid "7f03e21b-fba2-5b1e-bbaa-ba00d1112786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282783} {:answer/id #uuid "c372c923-e6e7-5ef4-8346-000c6e272e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282783} :answer-field/id #uuid "533cc9b6-1b67-5a24-ae39-2f81ecaf9c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64116d30-10df-5392-b872-327ec4131913" :question/math-academy-id "q-163642" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det(\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n$\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4xy-3{y}^{2} & 2{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{bmatrix},$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4xy-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}-6xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=4x-6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-3{y}^{2})=4x-6y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-6xy)=-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4y & 4x-6y \\\\ 4x-6y & -6x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{\\boldsymbol}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=4\\cdot (-6)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)=-28.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f0dacf22-0476-588e-ab05-d59e8b40b421"}] :activity/title "The Second Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526493}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125630} :activity/id #uuid "304b28d7-9caa-5437-822d-7b03dc2aef7f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125485 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125816 :tutorial/content "So, given a stationary critical point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R},$ how do we classify it?\n\nOne way is to consider the Taylor expansion of $f$ about $\\mathbf{a}{:}$\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a}$ is a stationary point, we have $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ This gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) = f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\cdots.\n$$\n\nLet's assume that $\\mathbf x$ is close to $\\mathbf a,$ $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0,$ and that the terms of cubic order and higher are sufficiently small. Then, we have\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a})  + \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is an $n \\times n$ *symmetric* matrix. Thus, the term\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nis a *quadratic form*.\n\nTherefore, we note the following:\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, this quadratic form is always positive, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) > 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) > f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *minimum*.\n\n- If $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, this quadratic form is always negative, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf{a}) \\approx \\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) < 0\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf(\\mathbf{x}) < f(\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nfor all $\\mathbf{x}$ sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{a}.$ Hence, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local *maximum.*\n\n- If this quadratic form is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point.\n\nThe assumption $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0$ is important. If $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) =0,$ then the quadratic form is zero along some line through $\\mathbf a$ or zero *everywhere* in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf a.$ In such cases, we cannot ignore the higher-order terms and must use another method to classify the critical point.\n\nIn the work that follows, we will only consider cases where $\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)\\right) \\neq 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3cedcd0c-77c7-5789-b76d-7ae3307c237b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4263 :tutorial/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Taylor Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "f3f90cb9-acd8-5630-859d-3bd37e502189" :step/math-academy-id 25915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125486 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125817 :tutorial/content "Recall that the *derivative* of a multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/b5/b5dfe5fbad89ef3c6231dafe40d719be1faf007f782342abd6475756eda97b1e.svg)\n\nAnalogously with functions of one variable, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ is a **critical point** of $f$ if $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ or does not exist.\n\n- Critical points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ are known as **stationary points.**\n\nThe local extrema of a function $f$ occur at the critical points. So, we can find the local extrema of $f$ by determining the stationary points *and* the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ does not exist and classify them accordingly (we'll discuss how to do this shortly). \n\nNot all of the solutions of $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ give rise to local extrema! Solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ that *do not* give rise to local extreme values are called **saddle points**.\n\nFor example, consider the hyperbolic paraboloid \n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/cea092da684db6b055c78369fcb987c3577a027ef0d8f03d598e115e85775ff5.png)\n\nIt's easy to check that $\\boldsymbol f'(0,0) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and so $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f.$ This point is a saddle point. Note that the surface curves up in one direction (the red curve) and down in a different direction (the green curve), resembling a horse riding saddle.\n\nIn any neighborhood of a saddle point $\\mathbf a,$ there are points $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'$ such that $\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}'-\\mathbf{a}$ are orthogonal and they satisfy\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) - f(\\mathbf a) >0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad f(\\mathbf{x}') - f(\\mathbf a) <0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60d15795-6c7a-5ec9-806d-625f5d8c5c85" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4264 :tutorial/title "Saddle Points"} :step/id #uuid "42eecd67-0de9-5101-84f7-5ef560683d60" :step/math-academy-id 25917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125485}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125487 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124570 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124379 :question/id #uuid "c6938151-ab77-5288-ba08-156dc942ddec" :question/math-academy-id "e-7030"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284418} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434764}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Sylvester's Criterion"} :step/id #uuid "98e2abbe-ffee-53fc-af20-297624f9d77a" :step/math-academy-id 25918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124646 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124209 :question/id #uuid "137818ca-6c48-5ae1-8788-2015f7890ad5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12460"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048164}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433378} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433379} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434762}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Global Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "1bf38d19-ac54-50d2-9456-e7e1e26fc0a6" :step/math-academy-id 44845 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125628}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124210 :question/id #uuid "976e4ec4-93f9-57a4-9f2b-2116e09fed22" :question/math-academy-id "e-12461"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445198}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "4b1acac2-a584-5026-9cb7-d5e4ae42f3ee" :step/math-academy-id 44846 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125486}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125873 :tutorial/content "Now, recall the definition of local extrema for single-variable functions:\n\n- A function $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\geq f(a)$ for all $x$-values in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\n- A function $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\leq f(a)$ for all $x$-values in a neighborhood of $a.$\n\nOn the function's graph, a local minimum is the lowest point of a valley, and a local maximum is the highest point of a peak.\n\n![](images/ba/ba5be082363bd963514baa6b1a777f02098d53a3b0a80ddc2fad231e4669ebd7.png)\n\nWe can generalize the concept of local extrema to multivariable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ as follows:\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a local minimum at the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x}$ in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a local maximum at the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x}$ in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\nIn particular, for a function $f: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R},$ local minima and maxima are the lowest points in valleys and highest points in peaks, respectively, in the graph $z=f(x,y)$ of the function.\n\n![](images/74/74d04165fd3bd5cd24ae82351bbf840e7d549b272c3deea16671dbfcd40bc75a.png)\n\nFor the points $A$ and $B,$ we can take neighborhoods of the $(x,y)$-values at these points such that $A$ and $B$ are the lowest points on the graph in these neighborhoods. Hence, they are local minima.\n\nSimilarly, for the points $C$ and $D,$ we can take neighborhoods of the $(x,y)$-values at these points such that $C$ and $D$ are the highest points on the graph in these neighborhoods. Hence, they are local maxima.\n\n**Note**: For the point $E,$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_E,y_E) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ or $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$, the point $E$ satisfies the conditions for a local minimum *and* a local maximum.\n\nFinally, the points $F$ and $G$ are neither local minima nor local maxima. Notice that any neighborhood of $F$ will contain both points that lie higher than $F$ and lower than $F$ on the graph (similarly, for the point $G$)." :tutorial/id #uuid "fc6789c5-925e-54f4-9a5a-bfb179e77937" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7752 :tutorial/title "Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"} :step/id #uuid "086acac3-3967-5f92-bf6e-ae6524a36328" :step/math-academy-id 44870 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125629 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124211 :question/id #uuid "94c54743-ba74-51c5-9c59-777125bb76f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12465"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048883}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Local Extrema Using Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "ab662d13-5448-5cfa-8e5b-f6bf4d245b75" :step/math-academy-id 44871 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125487}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125874 :tutorial/content "Recall the following definitions for single-variable functions:\n\n- A function $f(x)$ over the domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a global minimum point at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\geq f(a)$ for all $x \\in D.$\n\n- A function $f(x)$ over the domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ has a global maximum point at $x=a$ if $f(x) \\leq f(a)$ for all $x \\in D.$\n\nOn the function's graph, the global minimum and maximum are the lowest and highest points, respectively.\n\n![](images/63/633526565293f480a6a5fc45b87a70d38e946ae0f72a925f0074776e201a43a4.png)\n\nWe can generalize this concept to multivariable functions $f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R.$\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a **global minimum point** at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\geq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x} \\in D.$ The value $f(\\mathbf{a})$ is the **global minimum** of the function, the smallest value the function attains on its domain.\n\n- A multivariable function $f(\\mathbf{x})$ with domain $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ has a **global maximum point** at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a} \\in D$ if $f(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq f(\\mathbf{a})$ for all points $\\mathbf{x} \\in D.$ The value $f(\\mathbf{a})$ is the **global maximum** of the function, the largest value the function attains on its domain.\n\nIn particular, for a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^2 \\to \\mathbb{R},$ the global minimum and maximum are the lowest and highest points on the graph $z=f(x,y),$ respectively.\n\n![](images/de/de4cfc96fdce210430291999e5997f4a0c39ed1fbe63c3848b61508ca3f04114.png)\n\nFor the function shown above:\n\n- The minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = -2,$ so the global minimum is $-2,$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y) = (1,-1).$ Therefore the global minimum point is $(1,-1,-2).$\n\n- Similarly, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y) = (1,1).$ Therefore the global maximum point is $(1,1,2).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c6c71de7-b70c-5b7f-a566-143fd897ff59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7753 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d367e806-9c4c-5d98-bc43-97c56882eee8" :step/math-academy-id 44876 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125625}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125885 :tutorial/content "To summarize, the point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a stationary point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf 0^T,$ and we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point" :tutorial/id #uuid "ab23ca4b-1bff-5c96-a4d2-62b72b8adff2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7852 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Key Results"} :step/id #uuid "a55eef78-6bcb-5da4-be6c-3164a273066c" :step/math-academy-id 45322 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125629}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124736}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445202} :activity/id #uuid "632f0c8c-4357-5396-aad3-4401d3471d8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445199 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367874 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99f22169-b8a2-5fb3-aca1-3e5599415ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876} {:answer/id #uuid "abbaa7f2-5259-5b5a-bebd-07f255b181e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367877} {:answer/id #uuid "f674340e-9bc4-5748-9997-f0404db95ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367878} {:answer/id #uuid "383240c1-34bf-5387-adaf-b9355e4cf27e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367879} {:answer/id #uuid "0e483432-9f14-537e-b0dd-61a175cc1645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367880}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367876} :answer-field/id #uuid "be669623-0526-55a8-9e58-06217378cfec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1f0502ee-0a83-5915-919b-cb8a0333bfec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163775" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-3<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-9<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "40399da2-21fd-5f08-bac2-f30a1d0cf8b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445203}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445200 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118339a7-669b-5081-8175-449ccce667f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445192} {:answer/id #uuid "80185868-d8d6-5a9a-9b8a-83d741afd936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445193} {:answer/id #uuid "b251a48a-e4be-5932-a217-f573cfba9f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445194} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b3e9e9-2073-5961-8289-000100206e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445195} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b33820-f35d-5434-abf3-958f348e4695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445196} :answer-field/id #uuid "261ceadd-9e63-5d86-9762-c5e81f0679da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da2e1b4b-fd99-52e9-91fa-fb0d43968219" :question/math-academy-id "q-164157" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f4f2d616fa648fcd4663be22b32ab18662fffa8e31f9bd44f0dd3b6da99208.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch region exists for the point $A.$ Hence, $A$ is a local maximum point.\n\n**Note**: For the point $A({x}_{A},{y}_{A},{z}_{A}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{A},{y}_{A})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $A$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b16c2cb372280ec39915b0e8d4e396e68baec9a8e20edc5389ced079648836.png)"} :step/id #uuid "781a150f-a171-5196-8aca-b895a99d8faa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445201}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445201 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321b323f-56e0-55fb-b404-b19f30c4f96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,-1.2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282139} {:answer/id #uuid "cfae193b-da7f-5e1b-bf9e-4ef2f1177187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283501} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e29d66-dcd3-589d-8fbe-6a8511fdfa3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283513} {:answer/id #uuid "f4af8296-0c7f-540f-a7a3-bb0a43f520a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283827} {:answer/id #uuid "fadd78d8-5eef-5833-8275-ca89bd91ab2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1,-2)$ and $(0,-2,-1.2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283883}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283513} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8ed0cd9-02bd-5986-85dc-65dabf690efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284263}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "25f6d53a-4346-557b-a7bc-14685aa9ba47" :question/math-academy-id "q-164169" :question/problem "![](images/86/867fa59781c1732536e984398478996150c3d6b715c9d216bb82a35e0caf27e0.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global minimum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global minimum of a function is the smallest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global minimum.\n\n- A global minimum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global minimum. There can be zero, one, or many global minimum points.\n\nFor our function, the minimum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=-2.$ Therefore, the global minimum is $-2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,1),$ so the global minimum point is $(1,1,-2).$\n\n![](images/9e/9e2c084b2697ada9a957eec3629ac71aea150563a3af37b4f5d0b25e1921559a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7aa4c459-af9e-558f-a889-0dc28bf20ece"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445202 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124813 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488efa2b-10b1-5ebf-8034-0b0da5c882dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367744} {:answer/id #uuid "98cb67f6-d724-5948-9425-0c038ad2edeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367745} {:answer/id #uuid "15886344-1487-5168-91d8-69b1e7041eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367746} {:answer/id #uuid "eebf7d16-f087-53f4-ab00-09d21e4e2973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367747} {:answer/id #uuid "048f3316-57b5-5780-82e6-d17e9b479998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367746} :answer-field/id #uuid "740b6c0e-c3bd-5817-9e86-f793c37cace9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22b25e93-5d90-5fd1-8087-a5332a99d86f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162743" :question/problem "![](images/67/6715fe4c0f0b69044ee93da6d138a8d79cc9b509e7c912f79467d9f0b8e6d6be.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence $B$ and $D$ are local maxima.\n\n![](images/44/4464c66c00bc020668bef5e346caee352228403068be1334fc4997439140ae7f.png)"} :step/id #uuid "f285dabd-6305-5f61-b401-ef0b24f27f1a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445199}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445203 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475ce117-63c5-5ee4-b359-fc20c9c51651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401441} {:answer/id #uuid "cc880a4e-5176-5965-abb2-4d59cb691ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401442} {:answer/id #uuid "e2bd567a-a4c4-543c-81f0-2381cab6d5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401443} {:answer/id #uuid "56368805-72a8-566e-a53d-bb48170c39e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401444} {:answer/id #uuid "41a0973a-78e5-5b21-83f7-bcf5f3ddb6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401445}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401445} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8d87059-ea13-55bb-b541-23109862b322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2740d2d