:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401430} {:answer/id #uuid "27f2d2ad-7cab-5c68-bfaf-a31ab980901d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-2,1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401431} {:answer/id #uuid "d947cbda-24ab-58ef-b437-c399ba283d13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-2,1.2)$ and $(-2,1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401432}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401431} :answer-field/id #uuid "32d7053b-a2c6-58ee-8efc-3a03178f6bd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c163c8c-12fc-59a2-9e1d-9f264f8ad03a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163834" :question/problem "![](images/21/215b322e06280dd01e5858df9a17cd56673b5cdec69521decca205e03771ac21.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=3.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $3.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-2,1),$ so the global maximum point is $(-2,1,3).$\n\n![](images/d5/d5f948483a80f37257b285d48cd1e6bbf1d8a6ba9b66d1fea5edd865c787711a.png)"} :step/id #uuid "1354966a-bc66-58aa-a442-7d67b1cd669b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434756 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d6a8563-51e7-5f75-b1f9-5030c16e40a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434680} {:answer/id #uuid "671887d0-2723-5a15-b0f2-b1adf3728767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434684} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb013d8-4bec-59b6-be78-16c6b4278be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local minima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434688} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec46caa-3ab4-5a82-8cef-51349b889f2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434689} {:answer/id #uuid "c579e011-3bca-5b23-92a7-a4c2a45142bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434684} :answer-field/id #uuid "caaf0180-b775-51a5-8357-5dbad212c145" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "73e92678-31f3-525b-9096-a06391739cab" :question/math-academy-id "q-164151" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7429a2fc291fc9e14685208288f670cca341a260ac13b358c52d99c35e13b4f.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $D.$ Hence, $A$ and $D$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/b8/b8952aa59c9351c9723cd62f35073d46d7ddfd46afa9a9b3ddae7ce149065c59.png)"} :step/id #uuid "3895ce48-ecf8-5610-add7-df9917d32e51" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506626}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506626 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2683d2d1-d298-565b-a7c7-9a4e0ec1b79e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434665} {:answer/id #uuid "3674a427-b1f6-5423-9cad-a53248cc1526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434668} {:answer/id #uuid "3af27a0e-03ce-5737-81df-4b4e8b4ce792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$C$ and $D$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434669} {:answer/id #uuid "867414de-477d-5e96-ac86-a8231e8e612f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434691} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbd6041-7dad-5acb-839a-6742c3614749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The function does not have local maxima" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434727}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434668} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbf6504-629f-56a6-8d02-ea8b2cdc4409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c719ba47-2cb2-55e7-85ae-fa0a3ade984e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164153" :question/problem "![](images/79/79971602dd6834147c3db9361c94853a5a66c8e899978df3629d2841326fe62c.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the highest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](images/68/685c03b86db93032c8a79334c5fa46c561dd263a9aa4f52b9d7e6f41c3bb827c.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5c591abe-52fa-53e7-961d-0fc811191f5e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506627 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124811 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d51ffd-3c87-5270-834d-5cb8714970fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(-1,-2,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434670} {:answer/id #uuid "87e76412-257f-5583-a7e9-47e4ed2af022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ and $(0,-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434692} {:answer/id #uuid "acec2003-faf8-5414-9547-98be22326726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434707} {:answer/id #uuid "d277550a-a25f-5521-be5b-be206e9c7195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434721} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7d1370-1b00-5b14-93b1-c569a931408e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434735}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434707} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1e393d-2ed4-5869-b1f2-5c448966ff34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d4d9349-42bf-5237-9227-2e25538cab7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162705" :question/problem "![](images/89/897d0472039c499aee543a320f74b13bf1887f8b170ce1167a6c808d933ec676.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(1,2),$ so the global maximum point is $(1,2,2).$\n\n![](images/69/690d407982bfa9cde8fdce5b187006af1c24f9080a0cfea77e7551ca1d5cd940.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5ced1b4f-bfff-54bd-81e3-f29078d59fc2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506607}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a548b762-254d-5503-9bdc-ee9b3db0343c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465537} {:answer/id #uuid "5b890428-e714-50e8-a25d-194ffa0fa64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465537} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebd0423b-7a8f-573f-9d6b-7f4e7280a365" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de59ca97-1aed-5c82-97ae-0036990ece61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465539} {:answer/id #uuid "aba0580e-6d6b-54b7-9354-14b60eb284b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465540} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e7a461-81df-553b-9d92-c7e10bc4d0f6" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466613} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac0c9d0-2ccc-5127-9770-3602fe1c2d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465541} {:answer/id #uuid "fd97d7fc-8560-50f4-a5ef-982684deaed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465541} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bb3be7-ac04-517e-97e6-83c3aca76a8c" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9951cfdd-e926-5c89-b9b0-0744d28851f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465543} {:answer/id #uuid "3032c93e-22e6-5f71-81c9-c6bdd88b04e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465543} :answer-field/id #uuid "48758690-7815-5e22-aea8-571e2ab0e1ff" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "140468c2-8315-56a7-adfc-2601c4482a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-164150" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdfae6a5a321d1dbc235d06438847d5a0cc471fa4e31575f36c3967dca239824.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/82/82291f89bd32f3a3a20d39dfc89de91b2f673bec7c802cebf6d5fa3b4136ace1.png)"} :step/id #uuid "6cc3e502-2347-555d-b89f-52aa549d49ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506618}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505901}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520563} :activity/id #uuid "3294ea44-e0f0-5902-b1b0-d350a9b8ec0e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f9ab465-9b60-5926-9caf-66fa38e4437c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448472} {:answer/id #uuid "41ff740e-9971-5305-8cca-6d16b6176274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448473} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e509c73-9172-59d0-a89f-3107e3fd27d0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449276} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "208f176c-8dfc-594d-b8d8-8273c02ccbfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448474} {:answer/id #uuid "a82bd6fd-6532-53f7-b93a-eee32c232323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448475}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448474} :answer-field/id #uuid "06489517-8558-587b-98d1-a63c1fea14be" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449277} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0cf8462-0245-553e-8f2f-32ebff1011e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448476} {:answer/id #uuid "34965ccb-0322-5f75-90dc-bd64d3a0a98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448476} :answer-field/id #uuid "e666111a-27b4-5617-a072-b9aa01376d65" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b5496aa6-8f04-55cb-807e-38886a8c0e30" :question/math-academy-id "q-163722" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-12+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-24$\n\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n\n- **III.** $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}|=-24<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "647913e4-ab77-5ef3-a66f-ab77b352f8b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520584}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520570 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284422 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40769601-5e6b-5811-8d87-d4e245a3b44b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282244} {:answer/id #uuid "697f5a10-56de-5e96-8d49-0b219f74b52d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282594} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc1225a-208e-5576-8fa1-8495783e27ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282909} {:answer/id #uuid "994080d1-474f-5f19-920a-c11b2301f486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283021} {:answer/id #uuid "9a0fe6f7-98c5-5152-b74a-4001671d9b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283032}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283032} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebbb4b19-fdb5-5b6d-a6cf-ba581d730234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "45b6a4ae-c84e-567c-b717-cd9dbeea1d7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163785" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =8>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3-\\sqrt{2}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3+\\sqrt{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "70256a6c-5ad6-59e3-ab08-2d52513a56ea" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520582 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284447 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a548b762-254d-5503-9bdc-ee9b3db0343c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465537} {:answer/id #uuid "5b890428-e714-50e8-a25d-194ffa0fa64a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465538}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465537} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebd0423b-7a8f-573f-9d6b-7f4e7280a365" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466612} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de59ca97-1aed-5c82-97ae-0036990ece61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465539} {:answer/id #uuid "aba0580e-6d6b-54b7-9354-14b60eb284b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465540} :answer-field/id #uuid "24e7a461-81df-553b-9d92-c7e10bc4d0f6" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466613} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ac0c9d0-2ccc-5127-9770-3602fe1c2d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465541} {:answer/id #uuid "fd97d7fc-8560-50f4-a5ef-982684deaed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465542}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465541} :answer-field/id #uuid "62bb3be7-ac04-517e-97e6-83c3aca76a8c" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466614} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9951cfdd-e926-5c89-b9b0-0744d28851f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465543} {:answer/id #uuid "3032c93e-22e6-5f71-81c9-c6bdd88b04e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465544}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465543} :answer-field/id #uuid "48758690-7815-5e22-aea8-571e2ab0e1ff" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "140468c2-8315-56a7-adfc-2601c4482a06" :question/math-academy-id "q-164150" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdfae6a5a321d1dbc235d06438847d5a0cc471fa4e31575f36c3967dca239824.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhood exists for the point $B.$ Hence, $B$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/82/82291f89bd32f3a3a20d39dfc89de91b2f673bec7c802cebf6d5fa3b4136ace1.png)"} :step/id #uuid "87ef8ba1-8124-5cd2-acd6-4507d7bd5ac3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520584 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ed3573-aeb0-5cc8-aa92-2d155431b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281776} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1c7147-5d4b-5e81-833d-854efd096d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777} {:answer/id #uuid "194b6ddb-9926-5f0f-9190-d8ff2af941e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281919} {:answer/id #uuid "27c2e034-47f4-59cd-b78d-6e4055cb354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282029} {:answer/id #uuid "b540775c-a7f1-590f-8bcc-78b4486e47c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5a40b87-497b-52fb-93f4-bce3fac9446e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9c2261a-709f-5777-b889-b197f8b76cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-163773" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "8f9fcbe2-aa90-5f81-8960-a431bf2e3766" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520570}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367888 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79848729-40e3-58f8-aead-25a4bf9f91ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468960} {:answer/id #uuid "bdf43adb-e00a-52d6-9171-6bb57f6c2393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468961}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468960} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a2e7e84-4d50-5f18-bf38-f3d4119b4f61" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470092} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1aeab25-5623-5fb1-a33c-8659586a7265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468962} {:answer/id #uuid "000a5f85-f437-53f8-913c-2f859328fe33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468963}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7aae1-f019-57b9-9bd5-f09302e71e14" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e948e36-b6c1-5434-958f-e2bbe1ea693a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468964} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6e86d7-eddf-593d-8716-1c788f8c5e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468965}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468965} :answer-field/id #uuid "431cae4d-364e-588f-aeb2-fee9cea68018" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "61ffb698-9cc2-5e02-b6d9-afec7d1f3422" :question/math-academy-id "q-163790" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-7+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local maximum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5(11)-2(-4)+4(10) \\\\  & =-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-5<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}|=11>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-7<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c3de40d9-0906-5d15-968e-d7cd6767d329" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520582}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519988}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521595} :activity/id #uuid "e8924afe-1a47-5aee-af84-25e72799ddd7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284417 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09ed3573-aeb0-5cc8-aa92-2d155431b5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281776} {:answer/id #uuid "0a1c7147-5d4b-5e81-833d-854efd096d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777} {:answer/id #uuid "194b6ddb-9926-5f0f-9190-d8ff2af941e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281919} {:answer/id #uuid "27c2e034-47f4-59cd-b78d-6e4055cb354d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282029} {:answer/id #uuid "b540775c-a7f1-590f-8bcc-78b4486e47c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283262}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281777} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5a40b87-497b-52fb-93f4-bce3fac9446e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284284}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9c2261a-709f-5777-b889-b197f8b76cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-163773" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "4e9223b8-eead-5430-8672-05f220aed1df" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a8d6e4-83ad-5779-b6b1-8d3f2f9ddd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474478} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3a7e62-eb4d-5b61-9aa1-0d372465232c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef37d28-32ff-5d41-97ce-27cbd12aaa3d" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c7161a-6281-5453-ba04-08a2ff9730cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} {:answer/id #uuid "adc00843-8143-55a5-b0fe-475c44f606db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0324d3-98fc-5c1c-a873-f07d8e16c52d" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff8f7eb-17b6-54d7-ade8-e5cacfad8918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474482} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27ba75-5695-5a59-96ba-eacafc25acb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7524397-46db-58f5-a737-2282ef746679" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47992fdc-ac9e-5b34-9ffc-1e1720081dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} {:answer/id #uuid "e264ae91-5911-505d-a745-60ea3f527160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bce15c3-2af0-5965-946b-f94b71b680d6" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-164101" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)"} :step/id #uuid "5b4f1eb6-06aa-5151-a935-a33c43790429"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434687} {:answer/id #uuid "ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434708} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434729} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163774" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "5c8783e4-94f5-56ac-a41a-69d2ba5ab312" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434752 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48aa8c46-39c6-5468-85c5-ec68e86acbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434676} {:answer/id #uuid "63a1c505-6f99-5e6b-92df-f1c0f1d2c71d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434681} {:answer/id #uuid "9984c603-9561-5d15-a7da-151b72f56966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434699} {:answer/id #uuid "d563919d-d0e0-5990-917f-9b961dfd1ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434723} {:answer/id #uuid "ee820c78-33c5-521b-97ae-0bef262419cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434728}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434728} :answer-field/id #uuid "812379f4-7ca2-54a9-9137-3e6202ac33a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "463ee037-d37e-5536-bfb1-df9c529010e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163776" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1-\\sqrt{5}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1+\\sqrt{5},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =1-\\sqrt{5}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =1+\\sqrt{5}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "6003b5aa-8454-5323-bd58-1be79af915b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521644}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67ace240-ec76-51a3-9237-34363ff229dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(2,-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433358} {:answer/id #uuid "7c661f49-8f75-57c0-807f-bf1886abf88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2,-2.5)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433359} {:answer/id #uuid "927103a8-b10f-5218-a094-fe0cd4985992" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,0,1)$ and $(0,2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433360} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc9d090-f059-5d47-bb5f-8ea074628146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433367} {:answer/id #uuid "f027110e-c325-53d5-8b9d-70300263dd44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433367} :answer-field/id #uuid "8390ddee-9042-5713-b922-461ba822e40f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186433376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e0f0650-f17e-5833-8166-da3fd9367f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-163795" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a39696fcb202485b9a58839609ad807e43397fde8df965bed2824f6cdc307703.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=1.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $1.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y)=(-1,-2)$ and  $(x,y)=(1,0),$so the global maximum points are $(-1,-2,1)$ and $(1,0,1).$\n\n![](images/4a/4a818d3e3e3c6c6a90ff9e69d808803016114894fbc605de5d21c75817eb8cf3.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d17e39b1-6565-5f9d-b8bf-8cebdb011f05" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521599}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520758}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554705} :activity/id #uuid "80dffb9b-6686-5250-93b4-d26b471b47b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "092ff8d8-cf90-56ef-8c25-e605f251193c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474494} {:answer/id #uuid "f435ae19-d966-5bc7-9269-fe24671a9719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474494} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa760edb-c42e-5286-8483-d57d5dc10238" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a15a1e81-02c1-5e18-b7bc-07b26f9384fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474496} {:answer/id #uuid "2a16a913-6add-5730-88f1-0650356168a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474497} :answer-field/id #uuid "54555e33-802a-5a49-92b9-9f62666a56e5" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475174} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "457c64dc-56fb-5c01-a526-273fb7c1a2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474498} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb666e3-c28f-57c2-b34a-73ed59f5b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474498} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ab95613-7503-59aa-9a5e-45419ccd0135" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475175} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0812b1fb-a07e-5022-afd7-9a772e5b6236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474500} {:answer/id #uuid "af0f1167-9279-5382-98c3-8216e3f19902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474501} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc057c0-060c-5d7a-a86f-85031f906e13" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f6e52f3-8502-516e-a6ab-c65771746796" :question/math-academy-id "q-164168" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f3852805b0ae44433828113396fa63d0688ec06ae57886428815515e521baa49.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](images/f1/f10742ca92d2429c35b36cf77ae2d857774faba848a74291bbfa8e5ac71eeada.png)"} :step/id #uuid "0e56b147-81e4-5b76-ba78-f42888fc798d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434763 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9a8d6e4-83ad-5779-b6b1-8d3f2f9ddd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474478} {:answer/id #uuid "6d3a7e62-eb4d-5b61-9aa1-0d372465232c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474479} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ef37d28-32ff-5d41-97ce-27cbd12aaa3d" :answer-field/key "A" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47c7161a-6281-5453-ba04-08a2ff9730cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} {:answer/id #uuid "adc00843-8143-55a5-b0fe-475c44f606db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474480} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f0324d3-98fc-5c1c-a873-f07d8e16c52d" :answer-field/key "B" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff8f7eb-17b6-54d7-ade8-e5cacfad8918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474482} {:answer/id #uuid "3f27ba75-5695-5a59-96ba-eacafc25acb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474483} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7524397-46db-58f5-a737-2282ef746679" :answer-field/key "C" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47992fdc-ac9e-5b34-9ffc-1e1720081dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} {:answer/id #uuid "e264ae91-5911-505d-a745-60ea3f527160" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474485}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474484} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bce15c3-2af0-5965-946b-f94b71b680d6" :answer-field/key "D" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e433d738-c2a7-5a16-852b-741bb762e1fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-164101" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4ca398cfb365346962fc66ffecd7fb85390a9a98c49cb1926f6d8093a01c8bc.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch neighborhoods exist for the points $A$ and $C.$ Hence, $A$ and $C$ are local minima.\n\n![](images/02/0262a775dad79697ea888b6047e607c12ec804dbb734b72b2ca8fda54d6c130f.png)"} :step/id #uuid "7aed301f-ecb7-5892-aaed-84080d4f66c0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002ed759-21f8-5564-aa23-e5ae38d108b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf6cd21-6e6c-5f23-90a9-561d369a6b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434687} {:answer/id #uuid "ada293be-8d8f-53b2-8e22-8254f87db838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434708} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ef5c12-e171-59db-9f19-75d60d6fc0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434729} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc3a5a8-400b-5371-8e6e-5ad2e09e9946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434733}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434656} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9aa5f48-03a4-5e22-bfab-3a430f54be65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ae219904-d456-5249-bf4b-05599f3e75b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-163774" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈2+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1+\\sqrt{26}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1-\\sqrt{26},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both negative and positive eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1+\\sqrt{26}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-1-\\sqrt{26}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "90845892-a21e-5ed0-8f4d-4176220e88dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554743}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554721 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf8f149-6cad-5a5c-9d26-11b86bcaa720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465519} {:answer/id #uuid "6b04f584-4740-59cf-8497-f747191febb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465520} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddfefddd-b168-5878-87f2-f8f0e1876a2e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f9329ab-4f40-5e23-86a5-bf4d0b285377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465521} {:answer/id #uuid "5441ae78-27c1-5694-8618-d6f72b5ef496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465522} :answer-field/id #uuid "d143db0c-e1c0-5daa-9470-d0193838ae88" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12620dec-4540-5bfc-a97c-4be11c2d223d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465523} {:answer/id #uuid "13ec6254-73f7-5076-8506-15526b0e95bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465523} :answer-field/id #uuid "c993b7a5-1549-5886-b715-49e5a62df038" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "00b2158a-941e-5a2f-b68c-c230bee6df19" :question/math-academy-id "q-163791" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $det(A)=-19$\n- **II.** $A$ is negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local minimum of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ab83c715-96e6-5979-ad33-c4a45c733837" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554687}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554743 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e792ff8-af62-57d5-a566-a450e4d2c073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465513} {:answer/id #uuid "c06e501a-7fca-57b5-8fd8-00c7ba0828ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465514}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465513} :answer-field/id #uuid "7767a756-a8d3-5be5-bfc9-c38d9f8739ab" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d91f22ee-b903-5aa9-a3a0-e9971360e020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465515} {:answer/id #uuid "ddaa9832-b748-5975-984c-dd66a942f7ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465516} :answer-field/id #uuid "caa63e50-d620-5761-8485-c5faf4821dd9" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "476a495d-1c2e-59e7-a3f1-54bf4179f4b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465517} {:answer/id #uuid "6a781903-27e5-5a45-afd1-4d6f7a5fdce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465518}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186465518} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b937cb6-cf67-521a-8508-427cc88aed42" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c598e2d2-d26a-59ea-a0e5-a7e53e350ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-163789" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e73ff290-95f1-54f7-9f82-02df0d72039f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554721}}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553849}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557345} :activity/id #uuid "82d3ad84-5589-52c2-aae9-93e87f958df9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676 :topic/id #uuid "0a177390-6525-5d0c-8721-f7f75db39408" :topic/math-academy-id 1945} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124822 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "095fbc4a-1777-5a55-a970-420ada306ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448478} {:answer/id #uuid "9a99bace-ff30-5ed5-a0e7-4ced50a5bbb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448479}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448479} :answer-field/id #uuid "4017ed68-fcbb-59db-9e5f-e1de08b2d73c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449279} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "394fa51a-598d-52f3-b478-46d352cda2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448480} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e8a81d-7472-5c4e-a312-336416f4ec2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448481} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d74ca00-9446-532b-9773-a227a0971587" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449280} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23795227-e3c9-5931-b521-8ee6756b3090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448482} {:answer/id #uuid "f2439755-795b-54c3-ab71-3adc7e5c18ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448483} :answer-field/id #uuid "766f6f44-2888-5026-89cd-943039d2c099" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f4f668d6-a868-59b1-9e4d-3e52d3a52a67" :question/math-academy-id "q-163723" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- **II.** $A$ is positive-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "2209cdda-a848-5c48-8847-a397143e6652" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caadcd75-2894-55c4-9844-09b832086177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468954} {:answer/id #uuid "95a7a84c-bce3-5f15-881a-dbcea3b99751" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468955} :answer-field/id #uuid "a099ca7a-3e8c-5904-be12-6c4b93c0d187" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aae58fe6-7d2f-510e-8f4d-d82de11d3786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468956} {:answer/id #uuid "ae687bee-0042-50ef-b035-f59c08ad8cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468957}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468957} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec929f9-4d85-5417-b2ca-3a0683ff1a3e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39234e9c-2da3-5747-a131-1a128a6b9bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468958} {:answer/id #uuid "bf82c8a7-ab9f-5ede-99cf-894371a25d1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186468959} :answer-field/id #uuid "c14db63c-f2a2-5555-a425-0a6835e03736" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "883c276b-11e1-503d-959e-3a4c3a5e61c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163777" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${a}_{11}=-2$ and $det(A)=-7$\n- **II.** $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- **III.** $a$ is a saddle point of $f$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-2$ and\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|=-7<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "4a389a3e-5f4c-5685-9f82-4368b3602633" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557376}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1342b725-dc31-5b5a-99d1-77316495d1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local minimum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434659} {:answer/id #uuid "42eede05-2998-52b8-82a7-f2c9485a49f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434672} {:answer/id #uuid "6dd13874-5f64-5852-a430-9a9d7ca920b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "local maximum" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434685} {:answer/id #uuid "77644fed-85aa-50e4-ad64-f4f0dfaa2ac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434686} {:answer/id #uuid "ec57deac-58c3-5209-82ba-5d99ae0db2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "saddle point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434726}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434726} :answer-field/id #uuid "a84e77c5-ac0f-5d82-92c5-daa66014b1bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2a2b77d8-21b3-512a-8f60-975fa8c9b29e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163783" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-78+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}(x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,{\\lambda }_{2}=2-2\\sqrt{13}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2+2\\sqrt{13},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-6<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =2-2\\sqrt{13}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =2+2\\sqrt{13}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$"} :step/id #uuid "895228e3-df9c-57e6-bc94-cdfcb36433fa"}] :activity/title "Global vs. Local Extrema and Critical Points of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556413}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199335} :activity/id #uuid "d96c3f54-0a5f-5ebf-a90e-04722ee6bf25" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962 :topic/id #uuid "220386bf-4147-5f25-bbd8-b3b4f855f1a0" :topic/math-academy-id 4169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199487 :tutorial/content "We've spent some time studying so-called **plane transformations** of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll generalize our understanding of derivatives to transformations (or functions) of the form \n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.\n$$\n\nTo motivate the discussion, we start by considering functions of one variable. \n\nRecall that a function $f: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is **differentiable** at a point $x = a$ if there exists a number $f'(a)$, called the **derivative** of $f$ at $a$, such that\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nWe can restate this definition by applying basic algebraic properties of limits.\n\nSince the left-hand side, $f'(a)$, is just a constant, we can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} f'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a},\n$$\n\nusing the fact that the limit of a constant is the constant itself.\n\nBringing all terms to one side, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} - f'(a) \\right) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rearranged as\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to a} \\left( \\frac{f(x) - f(a) - f'(a)(x - a)}{x - a} \\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis final expression tells us that the difference between $f(x)$ and its *linear approximation* $f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)$ becomes negligible relative to $x - a$ as $x$ approaches $a$.\n\nIn other words, not only does $f(x)$ become close to $f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)$ near $a$, but the error between them shrinks *faster than* the distance $|x - a|$ itself.\n\nLet's see how we can generalize this definition to vector-valued functions." :tutorial/id #uuid "a9769637-9fcb-5829-817e-f82930d6d99b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7762 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "087cad91-5bec-5130-91c0-60421a04d402" :step/math-academy-id 44919 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199362}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199336 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198411 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198053 :question/id #uuid "ea202ce3-07d4-5324-9ee4-f5617b430772" :question/math-academy-id "e-12477"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049809}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434997}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"} :step/id #uuid "222e5898-b5c5-5d99-94e8-a487c966ad9c" :step/math-academy-id 44920 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199337}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199337 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198412 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198054 :question/id #uuid "ea76d21f-eff8-5634-975f-2b5bd24caf54" :question/math-academy-id "e-12478"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a818e04-786a-56b6-90cf-dcb573ccfa64" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048219}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510464}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Vector Function"} :step/id #uuid "9cc059b7-be28-5e30-9b41-3198b56bec94" :step/math-academy-id 44921 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199340}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199338 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198413 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198055 :question/id #uuid "db0be438-94fe-52de-9daf-60d1727ce7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12479"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e90349c-33ae-5b60-9d0d-7542dfebbbc7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050293}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510385}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function in Matrix Form"} :step/id #uuid "cc985165-12b8-5db0-9db3-3f6ef902bc46" :step/math-academy-id 44926 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199339 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198414 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198056 :question/id #uuid "f3540571-9159-50b4-a46c-4cf28a454602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12484"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f09fc28-bdce-554f-98f5-019d34542a13" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049012}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198656} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510466}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Affine Approximation of a Vector Function"} :step/id #uuid "2a4a2d6d-09ba-598c-aec7-fedd75d2da7e" :step/math-academy-id 44947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199340 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199488 :tutorial/content "The equation of the tangent plane to a surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0),$ where $z_0=f(x_0,y_0),$ is given by\n\n$$\nz - z_0 = (x-x_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(x_0,y_0) + (y-y_0) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}(x_0,y_0).\n$$\n\nLet's express this equation in terms of the total derivative.\n\nWriting our multivariable function $f:\\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R$ as $f\\left(\\mathbf x \\right),$ where ![](images/88/88f9f758bffba384670449973c5c89edae05c67268d2914a12801b21b60a319f.svg) the total derivative of $f$ is given by\n\n![](images/56/56dc34676135e9c35b9e08cff732715c5d878d4a19f4fee6625384460ed6ee5f.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/ca/ca5b0074c216a3235f015d7b96dece9ffc6fc50eecbdd0b5e97dfbd89eb446fa.svg) Then, we can re-write the equation of the tangent plane as a matrix equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-{z}_{0} & =(x-{x}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\cdot \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) \\\\ z-{z}_{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x-{x}_{0} \\\\ y-{y}_{0}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z-f({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}]\\cdot ([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{aligned}]) \\\\ z-f(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}(a) & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}(a)\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (x-a) \\\\ z-f(a) & ={f}^{′}(a)(x-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane to $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R$ at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a},$ in matrix form, is given by\n\n$$\nz - f(\\mathbf a) = \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "15747496-662a-5601-8abc-953fd6f5a43b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7764 :tutorial/title "The Tangent Plane to the Graph of a Scalar Function"} :step/id #uuid "67589fc2-05c5-57f9-9944-a77ae10109f1" :step/math-academy-id 44948 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199338}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199489 :tutorial/content "If $f:\\mathbb R \\to \\mathbb R$ is differentiable at $x=a,$ then for points close to $x=a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\nf(x) \\approx f(a) + f'(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nWe have a similar result for transformations of the form $\\boldsymbol{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m.$\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ then for points close to $\\mathbf x=\\mathbf a,$ we have the linear approximation\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf x) \\approx \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf a) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this approximation for $\\boldsymbol f$ is affine. Thus, if a function is differentiable at a point $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a,$ it is **locally affine** in a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ \n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "4f9fb273-0149-5dca-a967-7bb027abab86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7765 :tutorial/title "Affine Approximations of Vector Functions"} :step/id #uuid "322a9f6a-d0e4-5c2f-b7dd-5ac1d040f24a" :step/math-academy-id 44949 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199339}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199362 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199495 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a transformation (or function) $\\boldsymbol f:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^m$ is defined by \n\n![](images/cd/cd9132d6a0e934349ae58a0718e6320cba3dd8ae84de7df2e3fb98b391256b57.svg) \n\nwhere $f_1,f_2,\\ldots,f_m$ are scalar functions of $n$ variables. \nWe say that $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is **differentiable** at a point $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ if there exists an $m \\times n$ matrix $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})$, called the **total derivative** of $\\boldsymbol{f}$ at $\\mathbf{a}$, such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{a}}\n\\frac{\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x}) - \\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) - \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n}{\n||\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}||\n}\n= \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, near $\\mathbf{a}$, the function $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is well-approximated by the linear map\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}),\n$$\n\nand the error between $\\boldsymbol{f}(\\mathbf{x})$ and this linear approximation becomes negligible compared to the distance $\\|\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}\\|$ as $\\mathbf{x}$ approaches $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nIf $\\boldsymbol{f}$ is differentiable at $\\mathbf{x},$ then the total derivative of $\\boldsymbol f$ is given by the matrix\n\n![](images/6b/6bd7bcfb4e48c1833ff57c614bb18f469ca0a17e8a6e29d730b9e940c2f58984.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We sometimes refer to $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf x)$ simply as **the derivative** of $\\boldsymbol f(\\mathbf x).$\n\n- For scalar-valued functions of the form $f: \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R,$ the total derivative is the *transpose* of the gradient vector:\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a) = \\left(\\nabla f \\right)^T(\\mathbf a)\n$$\n\n- For vector-valued functions $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^n$ of a single parameter $t,$ the total derivative at $t=a$ is the usual column vector containing the partial derivatives of the components of $f{:}$\n![](images/27/27651b3ca026cad63ee6cbbd88162d3ce85b9afc642986daf0ae76c6519bdc38.svg)\n\n- For functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R^2,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2)}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for functions of the form $\\boldsymbol f: \\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3,$ the *determinant* of this matrix is the corresponding Jacobian: \n\n$$\n\\det\\left(\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf a)\\right)  = \\dfrac{\\partial (f_1,f_2,f_3)}{\\partial (x_1,x_2,x_3)}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0be5f3-0f3d-5c1f-81f5-97faa1864dc1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7793 :tutorial/title "Defining the Total Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "19886282-4967-5610-ab9e-7c0b0a12ce44" :step/math-academy-id 45103 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199336}}] :activity/title "The Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198497}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442221} :activity/id #uuid "fe2bd770-056c-540f-8c4f-31dd27239fe4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962 :topic/id #uuid "220386bf-4147-5f25-bbd8-b3b4f855f1a0" :topic/math-academy-id 4169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442219 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78256e55-f9c0-5f25-9bce-75879edc9fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367785} {:answer/id #uuid "ba371be4-a3c4-507f-8f66-4f2055137231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367786} {:answer/id #uuid "f4193f0d-676e-5937-aff3-0523d50d7633" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367787} {:answer/id #uuid "14823d05-96e6-5666-b344-a9dccb8d3e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367788} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5b3af7-e5e3-5a46-ba00-2dda1f55acee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367786} :answer-field/id #uuid "c371aaec-d4e5-5c39-a2b8-4eb96f2445c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f9d94cbd-ee2b-5982-acfb-18c8cc38370a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163073" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+1})=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2xy}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1cc56c04-dae4-5804-9f0a-0ad43a3a6deb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442223}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b3a6d3-2922-5952-9dd5-688afdd0e22e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442212} {:answer/id #uuid "3b47b2e0-7b7d-5338-9d25-ced74e84a6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442213} {:answer/id #uuid "5f1bfc1c-98a1-5b2b-8c25-93072e2631fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442214} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4d1f97-e95b-5ef1-8ea5-17d2a475dba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442215} {:answer/id #uuid "f115dc76-a7c4-5fcd-bfa9-03dec29e7b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442216}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442212} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d03c68-c527-56d7-8eec-20b909744859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "afc538b7-6818-5bf5-a45c-82af4af91e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-163159" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at  $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 1 \\\\ 1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}})=2{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}})={e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}})={e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}})=-2{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} & {e}^{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}} \\\\ {e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}} & -2{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n1+2+2=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "637340f0-b178-535d-bbe7-0f6d08053242"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198632 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b7dff74-eeb0-54c9-95ba-defe97065cc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & 2xy \\\\ {e}^{xy} & {e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367750} {:answer/id #uuid "82fd6625-ea85-5419-85d3-22e9978481b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2x \\\\ x{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367751} {:answer/id #uuid "5b34f347-2c05-5019-ad3b-34112b7f4944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367752} {:answer/id #uuid "7ef161a2-01f0-5905-9cd2-8d7b14170530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2y \\\\ y{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367753} {:answer/id #uuid "1e72e728-ff54-51f3-8370-ceab23a2187a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}y{e}^{xy} & 2x \\\\ 2y & x{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367754}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367752} :answer-field/id #uuid "407cdfc7-28c1-5a4b-b5bf-103c57e48a70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcfc99a4-cdcc-57b4-bd0a-78e0c8273abc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162941" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy \\\\ {e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})=y{e}^{xy},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy})=x{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ y{e}^{xy} & x{e}^{xy}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "94cb74fe-b2ab-55cb-8a35-96648021144a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442219}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401405 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0982cdc8-9442-57fe-b7fa-346753e57354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401407} {:answer/id #uuid "d02b2ee1-2d41-522a-96b3-adfd227f36c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401408} {:answer/id #uuid "9b952bee-251b-539e-8f7d-67e73938f6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401409} {:answer/id #uuid "03a4583d-e5ff-5e79-9be7-41b39f117fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401410} {:answer/id #uuid "71423d40-798c-5652-a081-532957655dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401410} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bc9a4e2-1d70-54d1-8a2a-b5a3fd7bf284" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e019c258-2f09-55a3-ab9c-d440f05baea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163092" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy \\\\ {x}^{2}-y \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & -1 \\\\ \\bbox[3pt,border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}} & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ 2x & -1 \\\\ y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$"} :step/id #uuid "d9a9ad4c-0b21-57f2-b0c7-24b864737653" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442220}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442223 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253a5862-fe76-59bf-bc9f-e5b4c2c81cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278922} {:answer/id #uuid "38badd4c-30f8-554d-b11b-dbcc78a85dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279087} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9f3b1d-5608-5c20-993b-fb7acb5da074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} {:answer/id #uuid "a4996288-fed0-5a92-95cf-6f46d536635d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280051} {:answer/id #uuid "d76a9632-4cec-5ffa-99d2-34ae09bdb959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c588e84a-c201-5164-ad12-e6f62f15b38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f8309ca-8cff-5376-85e1-fbcd4f2e85f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163075" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} & \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-2=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2+4+1=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ef05c0b5-9fcd-53ae-8c36-9e2f672c2758" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186442222}}] :activity/title "The Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186442211}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521653} :activity/id #uuid "35359baf-e17b-5fa9-b523-ce56f1275bdd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962 :topic/id #uuid "220386bf-4147-5f25-bbd8-b3b4f855f1a0" :topic/math-academy-id 4169} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253a5862-fe76-59bf-bc9f-e5b4c2c81cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278922} {:answer/id #uuid "38badd4c-30f8-554d-b11b-dbcc78a85dd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279087} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9f3b1d-5608-5c20-993b-fb7acb5da074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} {:answer/id #uuid "a4996288-fed0-5a92-95cf-6f46d536635d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280051} {:answer/id #uuid "d76a9632-4cec-5ffa-99d2-34ae09bdb959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280496}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279590} :answer-field/id #uuid "c588e84a-c201-5164-ad12-e6f62f15b38b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f8309ca-8cff-5376-85e1-fbcd4f2e85f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163075" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x},\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{y}{{cos}^{2}⁡x} & \\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-2=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n2+4+1=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0a2c83e4-ab25-57ae-8fa0-093c7c02ee67" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1289cc01-8ba3-55fb-aa94-7b7a7f9f74fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $y,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278763} {:answer/id #uuid "6f68142b-0df6-5d56-8ccb-cb1b9e66e70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x+2y,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279601} {:answer/id #uuid "7a2ce7c8-26e9-58f9-b87b-79976af9b225" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x+2y,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279681} {:answer/id #uuid "b65c521f-a49a-5aa9-ad23-75c7004ea65a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x+2y,\\,$ $y,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280192} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ee42bb-0936-5c03-8263-947d2468a2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$x,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279681} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fcc4252-ad1e-54d2-9309-0abe3e0f0029" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "cebdbece-6e08-53c7-9ec6-06388e9b98c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163112" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{3},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy+{y}^{2} \\\\ xy \\\\ y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe total derivative ${f}^{′}$ is given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y &  \\\\ y &  \\\\  & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFrom top to bottom, what are the missing entries?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{y}^{2})=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{y}^{2})=x+2y, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)=x, \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y & x+2y \\\\ y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the missing entries are $x+2y,\\,$ $x,\\,$ and $\\frac{1}{x}.$"} :step/id #uuid "10ea3773-466b-5163-9e1a-1748572301f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af21593-02cc-57a6-aa4c-0390054f5a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278838} {:answer/id #uuid "2036a02a-4ad1-56ae-b604-0656417d68a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278888} {:answer/id #uuid "61b856ac-a2f0-5c36-af0e-618709b441ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279481} {:answer/id #uuid "80116306-7dfa-5f3e-8b63-836f56c0da4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279741} {:answer/id #uuid "9533efc1-c872-59e3-b4c7-c46a879fd90b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-2\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279918}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279918} :answer-field/id #uuid "91a06bec-605b-59f6-8376-c36db871465f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4537e764-1c1e-55e1-b386-3aa2a68f9d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163158" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the linear approximation of $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{bmatrix}),\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}))=2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}))=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{2}^{2}))=0,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{2}^{2}))=-2{x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{2}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1}^{2}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{x}_{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{2}^{2})\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{\\pi } & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe linear approximation of $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\sqrt{\\pi } & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(x-\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ \\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n-1+(-2\\sqrt{\\pi })+0=-1-2\\sqrt{\\pi }.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a0a2e73-b200-5b81-a51b-23a65df802a3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cec9e4d-d65a-5c5f-8ffd-a37413d2ed7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}xy & 2x \\\\ 2(x-y) & -2(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279583} {:answer/id #uuid "9518cb6e-e0f3-56d2-89fb-cb0d06ff0cdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2}y \\\\ -2(x-y) & 2(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279917} {:answer/id #uuid "b3267934-0ac0-595c-91b1-da4bcc8e61df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ x-y & -(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280172} {:answer/id #uuid "f043d233-2594-5447-b840-36f606692595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}xy & 2x \\\\ x-y & y-x\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280691} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9d60de-2dd1-5c16-932f-bbaaf58eb7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ 2(x-y) & -2(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280802}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186280802} :answer-field/id #uuid "e743d96d-0f24-52ee-bc91-77076e175c57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2b79d2e6-eff9-5330-addf-23f64010a3ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-163097" :question/problem "For the transformation $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}y \\\\ (x-y)^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\ncalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)=2xy,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2})=2(x-y),\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2})=-2(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ 2(x-y) & -2(x-y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "92478ad8-e0b4-5b4f-870d-84971f3368a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a05723-547f-5119-9e38-64b224ba723b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279204} {:answer/id #uuid "5e568614-a847-537c-be40-d85446661d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279457} {:answer/id #uuid "90c0f44d-89c8-568c-b651-68638c9ae141" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279882} {:answer/id #uuid "d139d472-181d-5b28-bc43-9d61cc352b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280436} {:answer/id #uuid "e082d49f-a46e-576c-86aa-6cba2525945c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280577}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279457} :answer-field/id #uuid "7385f5a8-f17f-5d75-9deb-68d3efd689e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "723c2fc9-85ff-54eb-a36d-e043eb14286d" :question/math-academy-id "q-163056" :question/problem "Consider the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf the tangent to $f$ at $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{bmatrix}$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-∗=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{bmatrix})\n$$\n\nwhat is the sum of the missing values?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose a transformation $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{m}$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{f}_{1}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ {f}_{2}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}) \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {f}_{m}({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n})\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${f}_{1},{f}_{2},…,{f}_{m}$ are scalar functions with $n$ variables. If $f$ is differentiable at $x,$ then the total derivative of $f$ at $x,$ denoted ${f}^{′}(x),$ is given by the $m\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\, & \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy) & x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f$ and its derivative at $x=a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\nf(a)=0,\\,{f}^{′}(a)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent to $f$ at $x=a$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-f(a)={f}^{′}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f(a),$ ${f}^{′}(a),$ and $a$ in the equation above, we obtain\n\n$$\nz-0=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi  & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\pi \\end{bmatrix}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the missing values is\n\n$$\n0+\\pi +0=\\pi .\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1c2b0c6-b9fc-5150-94db-ebfecaf8e168" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521574}}] :activity/title "The Derivative of a Multivariable Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520749}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125649} :activity/id #uuid "ec39ccfa-c0bc-5c06-b1ec-f08770e57898" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125889 :tutorial/content "The **critical points** of a function $f(\\mathbf x)$ are the points $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist. A critical point such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0^T$ is called a **stationary point**.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x,y) = x^2 - 2x + y^2+6y.$ The domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2x-2,\n\\qquad\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y+6.\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](images/9e/9ea3f2d1dd0994be64602aeaffcf5d8424f52daa03844e21e65469f779d9b009.svg)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that ![](images/23/23faa4c01362cad11c2ab6ad19045ca44a09460da1c719c8212480f27f21c5b1.svg) or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist:\n\n- Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-2 &=0 \\\\ 2y+6 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system has the solution $x=1$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, $(1,-3)$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\n- $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so there are no more critical points to be found.\n\nTherefore, the only critical point of $f$ is $(1,-3).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66c2c3d5-6a0a-561b-81c6-ba81419caa61" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "59e0e71c-41de-59e8-af18-15eaf180ea00" :step/math-academy-id 45414 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124216 :question/id #uuid "593eeb42-56b8-5177-8a9f-38d08a5186c1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12584"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049962}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511549}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "c1edc476-8e7a-5e8a-83d5-126f47cd2bfb" :step/math-academy-id 45415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125890 :tutorial/content "Recall that, for functions of two variables $f(x,y)$, there are typically three possible types of critical points that can occur:\n\n- Local minimum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = x^2+y^2$ has a minimum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/14/14e8d23a4bc649257c2f7351e0d2c1d62e39a8cd8eed1467e65853ee121b836e.png)\n\n- Local maximum points. For example, the elliptic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = -x^2-y^2$ has a maximum point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/00/008803f43cb2161624f8ef295c70a125b8be64d93184928bd2176a6debaa9f85.png)\n\n- Saddle points. For example, the hyperbolic paraboloid $z = f(x,y) = y^2-x^2$ has a saddle point at $(0,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/12/1214b413073ad87a302fb1297cef47208a922e368d680a9f69ec8fb18d9ca8a0.png)\n\nLocal maximum and local minimum points are analogous to what you would have seen in single-variable calculus. \n\nA saddle point occurs when the slopes are zero in all directions, yet it is not a local maximum or minimum. The hyperbolic paraboloid shown above reaches a local maximum at $(0,0)$ along the $xz$-plane, yet this is a local minimum along the $yz$-plane." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe3c5f00-f720-5573-a01f-c37f0a3e447f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7877 :tutorial/title "Classifying Stationary Points"} :step/id #uuid "c5093fcf-6163-5240-9022-125813bd45bf" :step/math-academy-id 45418 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y &=0 \\\\ 2y-x &=0,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8248e727-7421-5535-ba58-b5ed98f7ac38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7878 :tutorial/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test"} :step/id #uuid "f1821258-7802-571e-a84f-0738986e608c" :step/math-academy-id 45419 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124217 :question/id #uuid "7c9f4b9e-be60-5da1-a140-08a3f183ce0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12585"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368791} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520514} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564527}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"} :step/id #uuid "4f254984-e6a9-5246-b99d-34252f2d1d87" :step/math-academy-id 45420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124218 :question/id #uuid "e5398577-d70c-56f9-9c89-4a9d4e8f1d45" :question/math-academy-id "e-12586"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61cadd46-5fae-51c6-8e5b-8114e48b12da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241041} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351245} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564495}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding and Classifying All Critical Points Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"} :step/id #uuid "9c6b333c-1f09-554a-8089-06e97c78f009" :step/math-academy-id 45423 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125895 :tutorial/content "Suppose $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of a function $f(\\mathbf x).$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that if $H(\\mathbf{a}) = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) \\neq 0$, then we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe second derivatives test follows from these facts. To see why, we first recall Sylvester's criterion:\n\nThe quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$) is\n\n- positive definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 > 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$ and\n\n- negative definite if and only if $\\Delta_1 < 0$ and $\\Delta_2 > 0,$\n\nwhere $\\Delta_1$ and $\\Delta_2$ are the first and second principle minors of $A,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice the following:\n\n- The first principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is\n\n$$\n\\Delta_1 = (\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}))_{11} = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}.\n$$\n\n- The second principal minor of $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf a)$ is the Hessian determinant\n\n$$\n\\Delta_2 = \\det(\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})) = H(\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can go through each case:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) < 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 < 0.$ Hence, by Sylvester's criterion, the Hessian matrix $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive definite nor negative definite, and we conclude that $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf a) > 0,$ then $\\Delta_2 > 0.$ Then:\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} < 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $\\Delta_1 = \\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2} > 0,$ then $\\boldsymbol f''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite by Sylvester's criterion, and we conclude that $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bcd9133-9025-5637-8956-90a3b84934a5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7886 :tutorial/title "Relating the Second Partial Derivatives Test to the Taylor Polynomial Classification"} :step/id #uuid "408587e1-74bc-5208-80a7-5977f2bc1961" :step/math-academy-id 45457}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124735}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520567} :activity/id #uuid "e57ebb2c-da7f-5557-85ec-a93c9bfb4862" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520056} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520145} {:answer/id #uuid "862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520187} {:answer/id #uuid "d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} :answer-field/id #uuid "06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104994" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(3,-3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(3,-3)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "11e5811c-fb05-517a-b6a3-b32833e3131b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520616}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caf69dd-971a-5d35-89cb-d9626dcb78d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520031} {:answer/id #uuid "23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520042} {:answer/id #uuid "2c29ee3e-35ef-5627-a600-d2e0619b9541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520058} {:answer/id #uuid "4396e4d8-a763-52af-afef-ded9f6e55a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520088} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0c6bef-702a-5967-84e7-559ce04a5279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520042} :answer-field/id #uuid "40aea253-69a0-5d33-b3f4-8166b4cfbd4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c1fd6c16-60ce-56fa-b03a-b1f876d9acf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37590" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(x+y+1) & 2(x+3y+5)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1 &=0 \\\\ x+3y+5 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n2y+4=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+(-2)+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,-2)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+3y+5))=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+3y+5))=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(1,-2))=2\\cdot 6-{2}^{2}=8>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,-2)=({f}^{″}(1,-2))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "6c650994-2746-52bb-b204-a4b55b445bee" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c27e9b4e-206f-5150-a881-d37dde9c4e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(3,1)$ and a local maximum $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434966} {:answer/id #uuid "d2284ea7-6bfd-52fc-b08e-cbc5dc03d189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,1)$ and a local minimum $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434969} {:answer/id #uuid "e02c6dc0-e019-5937-b18e-930a68cb954f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(3,1)$ and a local maximum $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434971} {:answer/id #uuid "e957c6c0-0532-521e-b2c3-2747f7ac1033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,1)$ and a saddle point at $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434972} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d5c20b-2412-5e45-a4e7-034876616c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434975} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8b2aa5-8481-5045-bb5b-59aa8a2d0aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0a336a18-c506-59a2-baef-25499733a8d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-81179" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2(xy-3) & {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(xy-3)\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}xy-3 &=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nxy-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9{(\\frac{3}{x})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{4}-81}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{3}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=-3\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{-3}=-1,\\,\\,x=3\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{3}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-3,-1)$ and $(3,1)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-6)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-6)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-9{y}^{2})=-18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2y & 2x \\\\ 2x & -18y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(3,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(3,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,1)=det({f}^{″}(3,1))=2\\cdot (-18)-{6}^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(3,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-3,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-3,-1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-3,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-3,-1)=det({f}^{″}(-3,-1))=(-2)\\cdot 18-(-6)^{2}=-72<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(-3,-1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(3,1)$ and $(-3,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "d8b6c8ec-acd4-54ce-8211-2be9c4fc4606" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "114d029d-432f-5d2d-a8e0-da4352b9cbe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520012} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1b31e7-050e-5530-973b-420e7f556047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520137} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1bf858-df10-5d12-842f-190930cca500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520195} {:answer/id #uuid "a61d9d31-5126-5495-bb34-7b540584d0e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520217} {:answer/id #uuid "da384a82-fb82-524f-a94b-1ea107ebde1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520137} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3b332ce-1536-5642-87d8-20b5ef880b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e71f4d1b-7ab2-518d-aa98-4d69ed0f6542" :question/math-academy-id "q-37592" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y & 3{y}^{2}-6x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-6y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-6x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6y &=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6x &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})}^{2}-6x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=2\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(2,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=6x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=-6 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-6y)=-6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6x)=6y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=0\\cdot 0-(-6)^{2}=-36<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(2,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,2)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,2)=det({f}^{″}(2,2))=12\\cdot 12-(-6)^{2}=108>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(2,2)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,2)=({f}^{″}(2,2))_{11}=12>0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(2,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "e28c95c3-e780-5712-a484-551662e9b69b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520605}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520616 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29711ca7-b3cc-53ec-bec7-d90a0aeb16e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520050} {:answer/id #uuid "3df5a0e2-1ea8-5fe2-a697-20a5c5faa39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520080} {:answer/id #uuid "cc0ee0e0-9a43-5369-b3d7-8e3c43d21d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520275} {:answer/id #uuid "ce672a55-9e57-56eb-a86f-0f1f3507439c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520280} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9f7794-bd99-5638-b872-bfdbc7829cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520339}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520280} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0046f1-2976-582a-85ca-24a28e09b21d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f7f93cc0-fc36-5de6-903e-61c89b0db59c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105213" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(-1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(-1,1)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & -\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(-1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(-1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,1)=det({f}^{″}(-1,1))=(-2)^{2}-(-1)^{2}=3>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)=({f}^{″}(-1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(-1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-1,1)<0,$ the point $(-1,1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "eb36864f-9d01-5aaa-9ce4-99ddaa5c782f"}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519996}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535118} :activity/id #uuid "9736e825-0def-59a5-91ca-930e623a504c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35563bcf-877b-5a37-b29d-40f490c08f36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$  has a saddle point at  at $(1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534367} {:answer/id #uuid "58b65e9f-4d99-5093-aa0f-4d2897f123b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum  at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534443} {:answer/id #uuid "87ca91c3-a26a-5635-add5-c073ea764a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum  at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534560} {:answer/id #uuid "c14d984f-72ff-59f8-bdd6-8c050d73fad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum  at $(0,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534697} {:answer/id #uuid "f0ae0c36-9a90-5251-835e-e8a464a23a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum  at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534560} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f7fa629-9baf-529b-99af-39063b4618f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac06f66c-0484-5c24-8d99-9d11ac2dbda8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81048" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "7680f643-a75d-57d6-8c87-a026935d52e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1caf69dd-971a-5d35-89cb-d9626dcb78d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520031} {:answer/id #uuid "23d7c23e-2e7c-57ed-a669-10ce3af13e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520042} {:answer/id #uuid "2c29ee3e-35ef-5627-a600-d2e0619b9541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520058} {:answer/id #uuid "4396e4d8-a763-52af-afef-ded9f6e55a26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520088} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0c6bef-702a-5967-84e7-559ce04a5279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520042} :answer-field/id #uuid "40aea253-69a0-5d33-b3f4-8166b4cfbd4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c1fd6c16-60ce-56fa-b03a-b1f876d9acf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37590" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(x+y+1) & 2(x+3y+5)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1 &=0 \\\\ x+3y+5 &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n2y+4=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+(-2)+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,-2)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+3y+5))=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+3y+5))=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(1,-2))=2\\cdot 6-{2}^{2}=8>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,-2)=({f}^{″}(1,-2))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2).$"} :step/id #uuid "968d2559-6e9f-56e6-9b9e-123351a336d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13574205-8e5b-557d-b506-8f6098c78c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534307} {:answer/id #uuid "13e49d2a-fab6-5159-b1c0-5a9b594d165b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,-2)$ and a local maximum at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534311} {:answer/id #uuid "5de0e24f-6b20-5c6e-ace0-a245f8aa51ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local minima at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534454} {:answer/id #uuid "7d90fa6d-adf2-5db3-b9b7-4dc01fdc1df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,-2)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534535} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc001e1-be83-5588-9961-a4a046536923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,-2)$ a local maximum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534688}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534307} :answer-field/id #uuid "b199132b-7b0e-5674-af57-8442f38abdba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9f714ecf-c225-5f6f-850c-327baa1dc6f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-81172" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4y-4x,$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=det({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "ce1efa80-0c1d-5262-b0b5-0d1381771d5b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535142}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535135 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520472 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b6b1671-0717-5482-8d52-d0c250e890fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520056} {:answer/id #uuid "3bef4c65-0c4b-5059-b575-1485beb2844b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} {:answer/id #uuid "63cd11be-33ac-578f-99a4-b5fbfef6ba96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520145} {:answer/id #uuid "862feb99-123c-5810-b1cc-80efe435e8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520187} {:answer/id #uuid "d30d25a2-0e79-557a-a62e-539de549b8be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186520076} :answer-field/id #uuid "06c9a2bb-e41c-52c6-8b2c-a83b5dffeaf4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60652dd5-fdd9-5974-9d65-a71597afa3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104994" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(3,-3).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(3,-3)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+2y-6 & 2x+2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4x+2y-6,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2x+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y-6)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(3,-3),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(3,-3)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(3,-3),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(3,-3)=det({f}^{″}(3,-3))=4\\cdot 2-{2}^{2}=4>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)=({f}^{″}(3,-3))_{11}=4>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(3,-3)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(3,-3)>0,$ the point $(3,-3)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "d671a181-8f1a-54af-9310-de7ad818f73c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535142 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af5318-875b-5713-b76b-c620f034eee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534308} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1d85e8-0bc7-546d-befe-bf2f6875ee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534489} {:answer/id #uuid "6da12e8f-9a72-519d-aa8c-c280dab33816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534492} {:answer/id #uuid "73c89adb-6ad9-5a52-805c-8e998406f119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534515} {:answer/id #uuid "9e958518-516a-5fe5-b01c-caeda1f4b877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534621} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca90436-78d3-5958-bb30-5ff0939b26c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22b41189-2cbf-5d3c-9c30-272b9cf4d5e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104972" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2y & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "f91e8e6a-a7db-5d9a-8fbc-5c958203d35d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535135}}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534258}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548126} :activity/id #uuid "bd9a456c-0340-58fa-a164-d6125ac2b9fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534985 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13af5318-875b-5713-b76b-c620f034eee7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534308} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1d85e8-0bc7-546d-befe-bf2f6875ee46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534489} {:answer/id #uuid "6da12e8f-9a72-519d-aa8c-c280dab33816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534492} {:answer/id #uuid "73c89adb-6ad9-5a52-805c-8e998406f119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534515} {:answer/id #uuid "9e958518-516a-5fe5-b01c-caeda1f4b877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534621} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ca90436-78d3-5958-bb30-5ff0939b26c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "22b41189-2cbf-5d3c-9c30-272b9cf4d5e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-104972" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-2y & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "59c32226-9848-5c7f-b446-707c5e49e63b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f6b037-a592-5081-aab8-180757dfb407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547461} {:answer/id #uuid "4cebeea4-ba06-5e53-be43-f6d3b1163a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547491} {:answer/id #uuid "63625bc2-1789-5d35-971e-846b92df64bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547534} {:answer/id #uuid "acc4f7de-f63c-51cd-b58d-6560706da8f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547694} {:answer/id #uuid "fc8a379b-a69d-592e-8528-13b53b904286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547833}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547534} :answer-field/id #uuid "9878ae59-6ea6-5926-9207-b1e7edaba613" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "698b79dc-de1f-5472-8b54-18da46bc32a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-37453" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & y{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(0,0)<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(0,0)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & y{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=y\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2})=(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2})=(1+{y}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2})=xy\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}(1+{x}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & xy\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} \\\\ xy\\text{ }\\,{e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2} & (1+{y}^{2}){e}^{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={1}^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "828c9822-48e9-5c25-a3de-311af96cc1b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "388c46a0-2156-5b94-b255-eb7d8c824a71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451815} {:answer/id #uuid "cf09000e-81b6-5c6f-8edd-ce69a7693399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451815} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3583c27-59d3-5f25-8f83-333d949fa445" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452755} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6922a55e-5db4-5898-a9ab-febe7e9d6d0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451817} {:answer/id #uuid "b92b6306-8101-52d7-bf7b-87232d6218fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451818} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5521bd2-b459-5546-a4da-6d72d1a17835" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452756} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6485ca2-abbb-5600-8cbb-d3f5625bd1ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451819} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad72361-a952-55a9-8858-ff6a491a4394" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451820}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186451819} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fb5ede1-7e8c-5d9b-b36a-a9a320103a42" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5521ee3b-fffd-5558-98b1-b81f3a08c9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-81164" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $H(1,1)>0$\n- **II.** $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- **III.** The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "bd1cf028-18c6-5036-a2eb-18834a5e4c1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399468 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "984bdcd8-65fe-53ce-9365-592fb416acc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} {:answer/id #uuid "6f09a46f-378b-5666-b31d-7d594024f410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399471} {:answer/id #uuid "6299c235-6bfa-5841-89fe-0705634d245b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399472} {:answer/id #uuid "2d302c69-b32c-57f8-ae7a-4cd8d92c0343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has local maximum at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399473} {:answer/id #uuid "d62b096f-ff9c-5138-8170-e9ee40a546da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399474}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399470} :answer-field/id #uuid "905988c5-8a51-5ced-aa99-8546923bfeea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7f004a40-028a-581c-ab25-3ac8bd7f4fe7" :question/math-academy-id "q-105281" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y &=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$"} :step/id #uuid "bec870e8-b940-58ab-a1e0-8e41adc74bbe" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2083fb67-822f-5c61-a364-c7c9d194031b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local maximum at $(3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238338} {:answer/id #uuid "2d567df9-25a8-5d23-8121-d47039a7f3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238456} {:answer/id #uuid "6248101c-5576-5662-a6e6-9e82ed11cdba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238920} {:answer/id #uuid "9657dc67-31a7-5307-b0a3-970644a18023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(5,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239356} {:answer/id #uuid "a885e205-57e3-5ddd-bba6-1c3c5468b96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local minimum at $(3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238456} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d816319-57a5-5bfb-bdbc-1674489ced59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9136d3e6-f2a0-5b9c-a6a2-efe0a42ad6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-105335" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x &=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=(-4)\\cdot 0-(-4)^{2}=-16<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(5,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5,-4)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(5,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(5,-4)=det({f}^{″}(5,-4))=(-4)\\cdot (-8)-(-4)^{2}=16>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(5,-4)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(5,-4)={h}_{11}=-4<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$"} :step/id #uuid "c3d9a002-9928-57fd-89bf-59d24c34b057"}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547306}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046668} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564547} :activity/id #uuid "48a27db3-de33-52fc-a27c-3fb1cd88a0ab" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051048 :topic/id #uuid "7d157e41-c50b-570b-8b5b-b8a5a0d03163" :topic/math-academy-id 1944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564544 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12262de4-ff35-5b6d-902b-cc063519daf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563994} {:answer/id #uuid "26e486e8-edca-5347-8954-f90b314bfea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564026} {:answer/id #uuid "3108d9ea-bf52-50bb-b021-c32e5e1cac89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564052} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4848d1-6bdb-54f4-845a-a67e55c75c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564207} {:answer/id #uuid "f422fa0c-c176-5bb8-b7d5-4e0f06f500ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564207} :answer-field/id #uuid "1df7676f-854b-544e-891a-356915ca7afe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564383}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90239ba3-7e56-5134-995c-b697d9dca4dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37587" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1565a6ad-3c1a-5468-a826-e4f531920bce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564562}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510844} {:answer/id #uuid "32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510890} {:answer/id #uuid "7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511041} {:answer/id #uuid "878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511062} {:answer/id #uuid "89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510844} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36799" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y &=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$"} :step/id #uuid "1a93e925-57aa-5404-85ff-161066e6624a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173c308d-bf5f-51b2-8d9d-eb671f780d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564000} {:answer/id #uuid "8033029a-ae28-5ac2-9529-3fecf69cf229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564164} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cae614-d634-5235-9e3e-6c34fd454900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564212} {:answer/id #uuid "c50acf6f-63b2-5f2d-8d18-c231d138b0b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564274} {:answer/id #uuid "d1710c61-c367-5d54-addf-e16baa633170" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564301}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564212} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad24458e-078b-5e2f-83d8-1060a660d14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "74b82340-7a7b-5e95-8461-8f632c1a4fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-81042" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "5b0addc6-5d94-59ee-ae4b-39e04b78f047" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "649a9222-abb9-59ef-b622-a080e0e367ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564127} {:answer/id #uuid "ab63172a-74c5-5c18-a8ba-084568e718c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564230} {:answer/id #uuid "c51de2e9-7a9f-5185-b3d4-4330e8045809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564275} {:answer/id #uuid "d42cc278-6a57-5ca0-921a-9ae2d0e2e661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564304} {:answer/id #uuid "f957faed-af43-5b2d-b1cb-4057f11bdadd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564304} :answer-field/id #uuid "c80c438a-7645-590f-8f18-5d9ec09d771f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "451d76ac-89a7-51b9-831d-a0c0d1695961" :question/math-academy-id "q-105286" :question/problem "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)=4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-6y-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4-2x & -6-6y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4-2x=0 \\\\ -6-6y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n4-2x=0\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-6-6y=0\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(2,-1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-2x)=-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-6-6y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-2x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-6-6y)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-2)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=12>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-2<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(2,-1).$"} :step/id #uuid "6438bf69-21dc-53b3-8c83-2912b62b9bd9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c9d9f0-2dbe-5ac0-8036-c9df0b172d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563977} {:answer/id #uuid "28023ab6-07b2-5861-aa38-d0641eeaa5d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564031} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf80967-03e1-5b5a-9f33-6ed0d8781d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564161} {:answer/id #uuid "c76ffc80-3225-5c67-acc7-073ef6b43896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564279} {:answer/id #uuid "f191d36c-e930-5f0a-8a04-f0c1f06aec36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564031} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f466cf-09b5-5527-b854-f04096edcf50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d69aae31-136f-59cf-90af-d2507ee77b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-104974" :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(2,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(2,-1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)>0$\n\nIII. The point $(2,-1)$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(2,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(2,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(2,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(2,-1)=det({f}^{″}(2,-1))=(-4)\\cdot (-6)-{0}^{2}=24>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)=({f}^{″}(2,-1))_{11}=-4<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(2,-1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(2,-1)<0,$ the point $(2,-1)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e2645ed0-faca-5f7d-bc46-8daaddd31c4e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564564}}] :activity/title "The Second Partial Derivatives Test" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563961}]}
